Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n160 | \n161 |     squeeze : bool\n162 | \n163 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n164 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n165 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n166 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n167 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n168 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n169 | \n170 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n171 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n172 | \n173 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |             if layout is not None:\n229 |                 warnings.warn(\n230 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n231 |                     UserWarning,\n232 |                 )\n233 |             if sharex or sharey:\n234 |                 warnings.warn(\n235 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n236 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n237 |                     UserWarning,\n238 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n239 |                 )\n240 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n241 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n242 |                 return fig, ax\n243 |             else:\n244 |                 raise ValueError(\n245 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n246 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n247 |                 )\n248 | \n249 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n250 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n251 |         if naxes == 1:\n252 |             if squeeze:\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 return fig, flatten_axes(ax)\n256 |         else:\n257 |             warnings.warn(\n258 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n259 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n260 |                 UserWarning,\n261 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n262 |             )\n263 |             fig.clear()\n264 | \n265 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n266 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n267 | \n268 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n269 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n270 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n271 | \n272 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n273 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n274 | \n275 |     if sharex:\n276 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n277 |     if sharey:\n278 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n279 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n280 | \n281 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n282 |     # convention.\n283 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n284 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n285 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n286 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n287 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n288 |         if i >= naxes:\n289 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n290 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n291 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n292 |         axarr[i] = ax\n293 | \n294 |     if naxes != nplots:\n295 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n296 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n297 | \n298 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n299 | \n300 |     if squeeze:\n301 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n302 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n303 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n304 |         if nplots == 1:\n305 |             axes = axarr[0]\n306 |         else:\n307 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n308 |     else:\n309 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n310 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n311 | \n312 |     return fig, axes\n```\n"}, {"content": "```\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py\nfunction: create_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nfunction: MPLPlot._setup_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nfunction: MPLPlot._adorn_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_core.py\nfunction: PlotAccessor.__init__\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/groupby.py\nfunction: reconstruct_data_with_by\n```\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 164680, "completion_tokens": 363}, "time": 62.56747817993164}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-16948", "found_files": [["sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF", "sklearn/linear_model/_least_angle.py:LarsCV", "sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_beta_divergence", "sklearn/naive_bayes.py:CategoricalNB"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF.fit", "sklearn/linear_model/_least_angle.py:LarsCV._more_tags", "sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF._fit_transform", "sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_beta_divergence", "sklearn/naive_bayes.py:CategoricalNB._update_feature_log_prob", "sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF._check_params", "sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF.transform"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the analysis of `_nmf.py`, the following locations are identified as potentially relevant for implementing an online version of NMF that supports different beta divergences:\n\n```\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1292\nclass: NMF\nfunction: fit\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1652\nclass: NMF\nfunction: _fit_transform\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 805\nfunction: _beta_divergence\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1411\nclass: NMF\nfunction: _check_params\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1701\nclass: NMF\nfunction: transform\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "7d93ca87ea14ef8f16be43d75cff72d05f48f7ed", "problem_statement": "Online implementation of Non-negative Matrix Factorizarion (NMF)\n<!--\r\nIf your issue is a usage question, submit it here instead:\r\n- StackOverflow with the scikit-learn tag: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/scikit-learn\r\n- Mailing List: https://mail.python.org/mailman/listinfo/scikit-learn\r\nFor more information, see User Questions: http://scikit-learn.org/stable/support.html#user-questions\r\n-->\r\n\r\n<!-- Instructions For Filing a Bug: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#filing-bugs -->\r\n\r\n#### Description\r\n<!-- Example: Joblib Error thrown when calling fit on LatentDirichletAllocation with evaluate_every > 0-->\r\n@GaelVaroquaux Maybe an online version of the NMF could be useful in big data settings.\r\n\r\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\r\n<!--\r\nExample:\r\n```python\r\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\r\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import LatentDirichletAllocation\r\n\r\ndocs = [\"Help I have a bug\" for i in range(1000)]\r\n\r\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(input=docs, analyzer='word')\r\nlda_features = vectorizer.fit_transform(docs)\r\n\r\nlda_model = LatentDirichletAllocation(\r\n    n_topics=10,\r\n    learning_method='online',\r\n    evaluate_every=10,\r\n    n_jobs=4,\r\n)\r\nmodel = lda_model.fit(lda_features)\r\n```\r\nIf the code is too long, feel free to put it in a public gist and link\r\nit in the issue: https://gist.github.com\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Expected Results\r\n<!-- Example: No error is thrown. Please paste or describe the expected results.-->\r\n\r\n#### Actual Results\r\n<!-- Please paste or specifically describe the actual output or traceback. -->\r\nHere I show the results for an sparse matrix as input of size 1M x 2.8k\r\nThe factorization has `n_components = 10` and each point is an entire pass on the data.\r\nThe current online implementation only supports the kullback-leibler divergence, but in practice\r\ncan be generalized to any beta divergence.\r\n\r\n![batch_vs_online_nmf](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11759948/53494592-69748b00-3a9e-11e9-8cc8-ce53ce3fe7f2.png)\r\n\r\n#### Versions\r\n<!--\r\nPlease run the following snippet and paste the output below.\r\nFor scikit-learn >= 0.20:\r\nimport sklearn; sklearn.show_versions()\r\nFor scikit-learn < 0.20:\r\nimport platform; print(platform.platform())\r\nimport sys; print(\"Python\", sys.version)\r\nimport numpy; print(\"NumPy\", numpy.__version__)\r\nimport scipy; print(\"SciPy\", scipy.__version__)\r\nimport sklearn; print(\"Scikit-Learn\", sklearn.__version__)\r\n-->\r\n\r\n\r\n<!-- Thanks for contributing! -->\r\n\nDraft implementation of MiniBatchNMF\n<!--\r\nThanks for contributing a pull request! Please ensure you have taken a look at\r\nthe contribution guidelines: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#pull-request-checklist\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Reference Issues/PRs\r\n<!--\r\nExample: Fixes #1234. See also #3456.\r\nPlease use keywords (e.g., Fixes) to create link to the issues or pull requests\r\nyou resolved, so that they will automatically be closed when your pull request\r\nis merged. See https://github.com/blog/1506-closing-issues-via-pull-requests\r\n-->\r\n\r\n\r\n#### What does this implement/fix? Explain your changes.\r\n\r\nImplementation of a mini-batch NMF with multiplicative updates.\r\n#13308 \r\n#### Any other comments?\r\n\r\n\r\n<!--\r\nPlease be aware that we are a loose team of volunteers so patience is\r\nnecessary; assistance handling other issues is very welcome. We value\r\nall user contributions, no matter how minor they are. If we are slow to\r\nreview, either the pull request needs some benchmarking, tinkering,\r\nconvincing, etc. or more likely the reviewers are simply busy. In either\r\ncase, we ask for your understanding during the review process.\r\nFor more information, see our FAQ on this topic:\r\nhttp://scikit-learn.org/dev/faq.html#why-is-my-pull-request-not-getting-any-attention.\r\n\r\nThanks for contributing!\r\n-->\r\n\n", "patch": "diff --git a/doc/computing/scaling_strategies.rst b/doc/computing/scaling_strategies.rst\nindex 5eee5728e4b9a..277d499f4cc13 100644\n--- a/doc/computing/scaling_strategies.rst\n+++ b/doc/computing/scaling_strategies.rst\n@@ -80,6 +80,7 @@ Here is a list of incremental estimators for different tasks:\n       + :class:`sklearn.decomposition.MiniBatchDictionaryLearning`\n       + :class:`sklearn.decomposition.IncrementalPCA`\n       + :class:`sklearn.decomposition.LatentDirichletAllocation`\n+      + :class:`sklearn.decomposition.MiniBatchNMF`\n   - Preprocessing\n       + :class:`sklearn.preprocessing.StandardScaler`\n       + :class:`sklearn.preprocessing.MinMaxScaler`\ndiff --git a/doc/modules/classes.rst b/doc/modules/classes.rst\nindex 9640f176925c5..a4217ad44cd76 100644\n--- a/doc/modules/classes.rst\n+++ b/doc/modules/classes.rst\n@@ -319,6 +319,7 @@ Samples generator\n    decomposition.MiniBatchDictionaryLearning\n    decomposition.MiniBatchSparsePCA\n    decomposition.NMF\n+   decomposition.MiniBatchNMF\n    decomposition.PCA\n    decomposition.SparsePCA\n    decomposition.SparseCoder\ndiff --git a/doc/modules/decomposition.rst b/doc/modules/decomposition.rst\nindex dd8fac301ceed..4f6a889473f13 100644\n--- a/doc/modules/decomposition.rst\n+++ b/doc/modules/decomposition.rst\n@@ -921,6 +921,29 @@ stored components::\n     * :ref:`sphx_glr_auto_examples_applications_plot_topics_extraction_with_nmf_lda.py`\n     * :ref:`sphx_glr_auto_examples_decomposition_plot_beta_divergence.py`\n \n+.. _MiniBatchNMF:\n+\n+Mini-batch Non Negative Matrix Factorization\n+--------------------------------------------\n+\n+:class:`MiniBatchNMF` [7]_ implements a faster, but less accurate version of the\n+non negative matrix factorization (i.e. :class:`~sklearn.decomposition.NMF`),\n+better suited for large datasets.\n+\n+By default, :class:`MiniBatchNMF` divides the data into mini-batches and\n+optimizes the NMF model in an online manner by cycling over the mini-batches\n+for the specified number of iterations. The ``batch_size`` parameter controls\n+the size of the batches.\n+\n+In order to speed up the mini-batch algorithm it is also possible to scale\n+past batches, giving them less importance than newer batches. This is done\n+introducing a so-called forgetting factor controlled by the ``forget_factor``\n+parameter.\n+\n+The estimator also implements ``partial_fit``, which updates ``H`` by iterating\n+only once over a mini-batch. This can be used for online learning when the data\n+is not readily available from the start, or when the data does not fit into memory.\n+\n .. topic:: References:\n \n     .. [1] `\"Learning the parts of objects by non-negative matrix factorization\"\n@@ -945,6 +968,9 @@ stored components::\n            the beta-divergence\" <1010.1763>`\n            C. Fevotte, J. Idier, 2011\n \n+    .. [7] :arxiv:`\"Online algorithms for nonnegative matrix factorization with the\n+       Itakura-Saito divergence\" <1106.4198>`\n+       A. Lefevre, F. Bach, C. Fevotte, 2011\n \n .. _LatentDirichletAllocation:\n \ndiff --git a/doc/whats_new/v1.1.rst b/doc/whats_new/v1.1.rst\nindex 3dc5799c228ad..0acd0612dd0c7 100644\n--- a/doc/whats_new/v1.1.rst\n+++ b/doc/whats_new/v1.1.rst\n@@ -288,6 +288,11 @@ Changelog\n :mod:`sklearn.decomposition`\n ............................\n \n+- |MajorFeature| Added a new estimator :class:`decomposition.MiniBatchNMF`. It is a\n+  faster but less accurate version of non-negative matrix factorization, better suited\n+  for large datasets. :pr:`16948` by :user:`Chiara Marmo <cmarmo>`,\n+  :user:`Patricio Cerda <pcerda>` and :user:`J\u00e9r\u00e9mie du Boisberranger <jeremiedbb>`. \n+\n - |Enhancement| :class:`decomposition.PCA` exposes a parameter `n_oversamples` to tune\n   :func:`sklearn.decomposition.randomized_svd` and\n   get accurate results when the number of features is large.\ndiff --git a/examples/applications/plot_topics_extraction_with_nmf_lda.py b/examples/applications/plot_topics_extraction_with_nmf_lda.py\nindex 48b69d710226b..38945241ab68b 100644\n--- a/examples/applications/plot_topics_extraction_with_nmf_lda.py\n+++ b/examples/applications/plot_topics_extraction_with_nmf_lda.py\n@@ -30,13 +30,15 @@\n import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n \n from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer, CountVectorizer\n-from sklearn.decomposition import NMF, LatentDirichletAllocation\n+from sklearn.decomposition import NMF, MiniBatchNMF, LatentDirichletAllocation\n from sklearn.datasets import fetch_20newsgroups\n \n n_samples = 2000\n n_features = 1000\n n_components = 10\n n_top_words = 20\n+batch_size = 128\n+init = \"nndsvda\"\n \n \n def plot_top_words(model, feature_names, n_top_words, title):\n@@ -101,7 +103,15 @@ def plot_top_words(model, feature_names, n_top_words, title):\n     \"n_samples=%d and n_features=%d...\" % (n_samples, n_features)\n )\n t0 = time()\n-nmf = NMF(n_components=n_components, random_state=1, alpha=0.1, l1_ratio=0.5).fit(tfidf)\n+nmf = NMF(\n+    n_components=n_components,\n+    random_state=1,\n+    init=init,\n+    beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n+    alpha_W=0.00005,\n+    alpha_H=0.00005,\n+    l1_ratio=1,\n+).fit(tfidf)\n print(\"done in %0.3fs.\" % (time() - t0))\n \n \n@@ -121,10 +131,12 @@ def plot_top_words(model, feature_names, n_top_words, title):\n nmf = NMF(\n     n_components=n_components,\n     random_state=1,\n+    init=init,\n     beta_loss=\"kullback-leibler\",\n     solver=\"mu\",\n     max_iter=1000,\n-    alpha=0.1,\n+    alpha_W=0.00005,\n+    alpha_H=0.00005,\n     l1_ratio=0.5,\n ).fit(tfidf)\n print(\"done in %0.3fs.\" % (time() - t0))\n@@ -137,6 +149,63 @@ def plot_top_words(model, feature_names, n_top_words, title):\n     \"Topics in NMF model (generalized Kullback-Leibler divergence)\",\n )\n \n+# Fit the MiniBatchNMF model\n+print(\n+    \"\\n\" * 2,\n+    \"Fitting the MiniBatchNMF model (Frobenius norm) with tf-idf \"\n+    \"features, n_samples=%d and n_features=%d, batch_size=%d...\"\n+    % (n_samples, n_features, batch_size),\n+)\n+t0 = time()\n+mbnmf = MiniBatchNMF(\n+    n_components=n_components,\n+    random_state=1,\n+    batch_size=batch_size,\n+    init=init,\n+    beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n+    alpha_W=0.00005,\n+    alpha_H=0.00005,\n+    l1_ratio=0.5,\n+).fit(tfidf)\n+print(\"done in %0.3fs.\" % (time() - t0))\n+\n+\n+tfidf_feature_names = tfidf_vectorizer.get_feature_names_out()\n+plot_top_words(\n+    mbnmf,\n+    tfidf_feature_names,\n+    n_top_words,\n+    \"Topics in MiniBatchNMF model (Frobenius norm)\",\n+)\n+\n+# Fit the MiniBatchNMF model\n+print(\n+    \"\\n\" * 2,\n+    \"Fitting the MiniBatchNMF model (generalized Kullback-Leibler \"\n+    \"divergence) with tf-idf features, n_samples=%d and n_features=%d, \"\n+    \"batch_size=%d...\" % (n_samples, n_features, batch_size),\n+)\n+t0 = time()\n+mbnmf = MiniBatchNMF(\n+    n_components=n_components,\n+    random_state=1,\n+    batch_size=batch_size,\n+    init=init,\n+    beta_loss=\"kullback-leibler\",\n+    alpha_W=0.00005,\n+    alpha_H=0.00005,\n+    l1_ratio=0.5,\n+).fit(tfidf)\n+print(\"done in %0.3fs.\" % (time() - t0))\n+\n+tfidf_feature_names = tfidf_vectorizer.get_feature_names_out()\n+plot_top_words(\n+    mbnmf,\n+    tfidf_feature_names,\n+    n_top_words,\n+    \"Topics in MiniBatchNMF model (generalized Kullback-Leibler divergence)\",\n+)\n+\n print(\n     \"\\n\" * 2,\n     \"Fitting LDA models with tf features, n_samples=%d and n_features=%d...\"\ndiff --git a/sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py b/sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py\nindex c280aeb2cf71d..c5f323d3c5d72 100644\n--- a/sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py\n+++ b/sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py\n@@ -5,7 +5,11 @@\n \"\"\"\n \n \n-from ._nmf import NMF, non_negative_factorization\n+from ._nmf import (\n+    NMF,\n+    MiniBatchNMF,\n+    non_negative_factorization,\n+)\n from ._pca import PCA\n from ._incremental_pca import IncrementalPCA\n from ._kernel_pca import KernelPCA\n@@ -31,6 +35,7 @@\n     \"IncrementalPCA\",\n     \"KernelPCA\",\n     \"MiniBatchDictionaryLearning\",\n+    \"MiniBatchNMF\",\n     \"MiniBatchSparsePCA\",\n     \"NMF\",\n     \"PCA\",\ndiff --git a/sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py b/sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nindex 443f9c4be1649..7623822ba5912 100644\n--- a/sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\n+++ b/sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\n@@ -10,14 +10,16 @@\n import numpy as np\n import scipy.sparse as sp\n import time\n+import itertools\n import warnings\n from math import sqrt\n+from scipy import linalg\n \n from ._cdnmf_fast import _update_cdnmf_fast\n from .._config import config_context\n from ..base import BaseEstimator, TransformerMixin, _ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin\n from ..exceptions import ConvergenceWarning\n-from ..utils import check_random_state, check_array\n+from ..utils import check_random_state, check_array, gen_batches\n from ..utils.extmath import randomized_svd, safe_sparse_dot, squared_norm\n from ..utils.validation import (\n     check_is_fitted,\n@@ -30,7 +32,7 @@\n def norm(x):\n     \"\"\"Dot product-based Euclidean norm implementation.\n \n-    See: http://fseoane.net/blog/2011/computing-the-vector-norm/\n+    See: http://fa.bianp.net/blog/2011/computing-the-vector-norm/\n \n     Parameters\n     ----------\n@@ -166,6 +168,7 @@ def _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta, square_root=False):\n         res /= beta * (beta - 1)\n \n     if square_root:\n+        res = max(res, 0)  # avoid negative number due to rounding errors\n         return np.sqrt(2 * res)\n     else:\n         return res\n@@ -493,10 +496,10 @@ def _fit_coordinate_descent(\n \n     References\n     ----------\n-    Cichocki, Andrzej, and Phan, Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n-    large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n-    IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n-    computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n+    .. [1] :doi:`\"Fast local algorithms for large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor\n+       factorizations\" <10.1587/transfun.E92.A.708>`\n+       Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. IEICE transactions on fundamentals\n+       of electronics, communications and computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n     \"\"\"\n     # so W and Ht are both in C order in memory\n     Ht = check_array(H.T, order=\"C\")\n@@ -637,11 +640,15 @@ def _multiplicative_update_w(\n     if gamma != 1:\n         delta_W **= gamma\n \n-    return delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt\n+    W *= delta_W\n \n+    return W, H_sum, HHt, XHt\n \n-def _multiplicative_update_h(X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma):\n-    \"\"\"Update H in Multiplicative Update NMF.\"\"\"\n+\n+def _multiplicative_update_h(\n+    X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma, A=None, B=None, rho=None\n+):\n+    \"\"\"update H in Multiplicative Update NMF.\"\"\"\n     if beta_loss == 2:\n         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, X)\n         denominator = np.linalg.multi_dot([W.T, W, H])\n@@ -712,14 +719,27 @@ def _multiplicative_update_h(X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma):\n         denominator = denominator + l2_reg_H * H\n     denominator[denominator == 0] = EPSILON\n \n-    numerator /= denominator\n-    delta_H = numerator\n-\n-    # gamma is in ]0, 1]\n-    if gamma != 1:\n-        delta_H **= gamma\n+    if A is not None and B is not None:\n+        # Updates for the online nmf\n+        if gamma != 1:\n+            H **= 1 / gamma\n+        numerator *= H\n+        A *= rho\n+        B *= rho\n+        A += numerator\n+        B += denominator\n+        H = A / B\n+\n+        if gamma != 1:\n+            H **= gamma\n+    else:\n+        delta_H = numerator\n+        delta_H /= denominator\n+        if gamma != 1:\n+            delta_H **= gamma\n+        H *= delta_H\n \n-    return delta_H\n+    return H\n \n \n def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n@@ -800,6 +820,8 @@ def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n \n     References\n     ----------\n+    Lee, D. D., & Seung, H., S. (2001). Algorithms for Non-negative Matrix\n+    Factorization. Adv. Neural Inform. Process. Syst.. 13.\n     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n     \"\"\"\n@@ -823,21 +845,35 @@ def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n         # update W\n         # H_sum, HHt and XHt are saved and reused if not update_H\n-        delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n-            X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, gamma, H_sum, HHt, XHt, update_H\n+        W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n+            X,\n+            W,\n+            H,\n+            beta_loss=beta_loss,\n+            l1_reg_W=l1_reg_W,\n+            l2_reg_W=l2_reg_W,\n+            gamma=gamma,\n+            H_sum=H_sum,\n+            HHt=HHt,\n+            XHt=XHt,\n+            update_H=update_H,\n         )\n-        W *= delta_W\n \n         # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n         if beta_loss < 1:\n             W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n \n-        # update H\n+        # update H (only at fit or fit_transform)\n         if update_H:\n-            delta_H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n-                X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma\n+            H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n+                X,\n+                W,\n+                H,\n+                beta_loss=beta_loss,\n+                l1_reg_H=l1_reg_H,\n+                l2_reg_H=l2_reg_H,\n+                gamma=gamma,\n             )\n-            H *= delta_H\n \n             # These values will be recomputed since H changed\n             H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n@@ -902,15 +938,16 @@ def non_negative_factorization(\n \n         .. math::\n \n-            0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n+            L(W, H) &= 0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n+\n+            &+ alpha\\\\_W * l1\\\\_ratio * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n \n-            + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n+            &+ alpha\\\\_H * l1\\\\_ratio * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n \n-            + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n+            &+ 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1\\\\_ratio) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n \n-            + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n+            &+ 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1\\\\_ratio) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n \n-            + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n \n     Where:\n \n@@ -1084,13 +1121,14 @@ def non_negative_factorization(\n \n     References\n     ----------\n-    Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n-    large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n-    IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n-    computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n-\n-    Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n-    factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n+    .. [1] :doi:`\"Fast local algorithms for large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor\n+       factorizations\" <10.1587/transfun.E92.A.708>`\n+       Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. IEICE transactions on fundamentals\n+       of electronics, communications and computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n+\n+    .. [2] :doi:`\"Algorithms for nonnegative matrix factorization with the\n+       beta-divergence\" <10.1162/NECO_a_00168>`\n+       Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Neural Computation, 23(9).\n     \"\"\"\n     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n \n@@ -1120,23 +1158,23 @@ def non_negative_factorization(\n class NMF(_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n     \"\"\"Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n \n-    Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n-    negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n-    dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n+    Find two non-negative matrices, i.e. matrices with all non-negative elements, (W, H)\n+    whose product approximates the non-negative matrix X. This factorization can be used\n+    for example for dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n \n     The objective function is:\n \n         .. math::\n \n-            0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n+            L(W, H) &= 0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n \n-            + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n+            &+ alpha\\\\_W * l1\\\\_ratio * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n \n-            + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n+            &+ alpha\\\\_H * l1\\\\_ratio * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n \n-            + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n+            &+ 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1\\\\_ratio) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n \n-            + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n+            &+ 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1\\\\_ratio) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n \n     Where:\n \n@@ -1325,13 +1363,14 @@ class NMF(_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n \n     References\n     ----------\n-    Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n-    large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n-    IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n-    computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n+    .. [1] :doi:`\"Fast local algorithms for large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor\n+       factorizations\" <10.1587/transfun.E92.A.708>`\n+       Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. IEICE transactions on fundamentals\n+       of electronics, communications and computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n \n-    Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n-    factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n+    .. [2] :doi:`\"Algorithms for nonnegative matrix factorization with the\n+       beta-divergence\" <10.1162/NECO_a_00168>`\n+       Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Neural Computation, 23(9).\n \n     Examples\n     --------\n@@ -1632,7 +1671,7 @@ def _fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None, update_H=True):\n                 random_state=self.random_state,\n             )\n         elif self.solver == \"mu\":\n-            W, H, n_iter = _fit_multiplicative_update(\n+            W, H, n_iter, *_ = _fit_multiplicative_update(\n                 X,\n                 W,\n                 H,\n@@ -1643,8 +1682,8 @@ def _fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None, update_H=True):\n                 l1_reg_H,\n                 l2_reg_W,\n                 l2_reg_H,\n-                update_H=update_H,\n-                verbose=self.verbose,\n+                update_H,\n+                self.verbose,\n             )\n         else:\n             raise ValueError(\"Invalid solver parameter '%s'.\" % self.solver)\n@@ -1729,3 +1768,628 @@ def inverse_transform(self, W):\n     def _n_features_out(self):\n         \"\"\"Number of transformed output features.\"\"\"\n         return self.components_.shape[0]\n+\n+\n+class MiniBatchNMF(NMF):\n+    \"\"\"Mini-Batch Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n+\n+    .. versionadded:: 1.1\n+\n+    Find two non-negative matrices, i.e. matrices with all non-negative elements,\n+    (`W`, `H`) whose product approximates the non-negative matrix `X`. This\n+    factorization can be used for example for dimensionality reduction, source\n+    separation or topic extraction.\n+\n+    The objective function is:\n+\n+        .. math::\n+\n+            L(W, H) &= 0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n+\n+            &+ alpha\\\\_W * l1\\\\_ratio * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n+\n+            &+ alpha\\\\_H * l1\\\\_ratio * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n+\n+            &+ 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1\\\\_ratio) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n+\n+            &+ 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1\\\\_ratio) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n+\n+    Where:\n+\n+    :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n+\n+    :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n+\n+    The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n+    the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n+    The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n+\n+    The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of `W`\n+    and `H`.\n+\n+    Note that the transformed data is named `W` and the components matrix is\n+    named `H`. In the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite\n+    since the data matrix `X` is transposed.\n+\n+    Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <MiniBatchNMF>`.\n+\n+    Parameters\n+    ----------\n+    n_components : int, default=None\n+        Number of components, if `n_components` is not set all features\n+        are kept.\n+\n+    init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n+        Method used to initialize the procedure.\n+        Valid options:\n+\n+        - `None`: 'nndsvda' if `n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features)`,\n+          otherwise random.\n+\n+        - `'random'`: non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n+          `sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)`\n+\n+        - `'nndsvd'`: Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n+          initialization (better for sparseness).\n+\n+        - `'nndsvda'`: NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n+          (better when sparsity is not desired).\n+\n+        - `'nndsvdar'` NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n+          (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n+          for when sparsity is not desired).\n+\n+        - `'custom'`: use custom matrices `W` and `H`\n+\n+    batch_size : int, default=1024\n+        Number of samples in each mini-batch. Large batch sizes\n+        give better long-term convergence at the cost of a slower start.\n+\n+    beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n+            'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n+        Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between `X`\n+        and the dot product `WH`. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n+        (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n+        fits. Note that for `beta_loss <= 0` (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n+        matrix `X` cannot contain zeros.\n+\n+    tol : float, default=1e-4\n+        Control early stopping based on the norm of the differences in `H`\n+        between 2 steps. To disable early stopping based on changes in `H`, set\n+        `tol` to 0.0.\n+\n+    max_no_improvement : int, default=10\n+        Control early stopping based on the consecutive number of mini batches\n+        that does not yield an improvement on the smoothed cost function.\n+        To disable convergence detection based on cost function, set\n+        `max_no_improvement` to None.\n+\n+    max_iter : int, default=200\n+        Maximum number of iterations over the complete dataset before\n+        timing out.\n+\n+    alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n+        Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n+        (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n+\n+    alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n+        Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n+        have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n+        `alpha_W`.\n+\n+    l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n+        The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n+        For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n+        (aka Frobenius Norm).\n+        For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n+        For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n+\n+    forget_factor : float, default=0.7\n+        Amount of rescaling of past information. Its value could be 1 with\n+        finite datasets. Choosing values < 1 is recommended with online\n+        learning as more recent batches will weight more than past batches.\n+\n+    fresh_restarts : bool, default=False\n+        Whether to completely solve for W at each step. Doing fresh restarts will likely\n+        lead to a better solution for a same number of iterations but it is much slower.\n+\n+    fresh_restarts_max_iter : int, default=30\n+        Maximum number of iterations when solving for W at each step. Only used when\n+        doing fresh restarts. These iterations may be stopped early based on a small\n+        change of W controlled by `tol`.\n+\n+    transform_max_iter : int, default=None\n+        Maximum number of iterations when solving for W at transform time.\n+        If None, it defaults to `max_iter`.\n+\n+    random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n+        Used for initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n+        'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n+        results across multiple function calls.\n+        See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n+\n+    verbose : bool, default=False\n+        Whether to be verbose.\n+\n+    Attributes\n+    ----------\n+    components_ : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n+        Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n+\n+    n_components_ : int\n+        The number of components. It is same as the `n_components` parameter\n+        if it was given. Otherwise, it will be same as the number of\n+        features.\n+\n+    reconstruction_err_ : float\n+        Frobenius norm of the matrix difference, or beta-divergence, between\n+        the training data `X` and the reconstructed data `WH` from\n+        the fitted model.\n+\n+    n_iter_ : int\n+        Actual number of started iterations over the whole dataset.\n+\n+    n_steps_ : int\n+        Number of mini-batches processed.\n+\n+    n_features_in_ : int\n+        Number of features seen during :term:`fit`.\n+\n+    feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n+        Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Defined only when `X`\n+        has feature names that are all strings.\n+\n+    See Also\n+    --------\n+    NMF : Non-negative matrix factorization.\n+    MiniBatchDictionaryLearning : Finds a dictionary that can best be used to represent\n+        data using a sparse code.\n+\n+    References\n+    ----------\n+    .. [1] :doi:`\"Fast local algorithms for large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor\n+       factorizations\" <10.1587/transfun.E92.A.708>`\n+       Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. IEICE transactions on fundamentals\n+       of electronics, communications and computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n+\n+    .. [2] :doi:`\"Algorithms for nonnegative matrix factorization with the\n+       beta-divergence\" <10.1162/NECO_a_00168>`\n+       Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Neural Computation, 23(9).\n+\n+    .. [3] :doi:`\"Online algorithms for nonnegative matrix factorization with the\n+       Itakura-Saito divergence\" <10.1109/ASPAA.2011.6082314>`\n+       Lefevre, A., Bach, F., Fevotte, C. (2011). WASPA.\n+\n+    Examples\n+    --------\n+    >>> import numpy as np\n+    >>> X = np.array([[1, 1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n+    >>> from sklearn.decomposition import MiniBatchNMF\n+    >>> model = MiniBatchNMF(n_components=2, init='random', random_state=0)\n+    >>> W = model.fit_transform(X)\n+    >>> H = model.components_\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    def __init__(\n+        self,\n+        n_components=None,\n+        *,\n+        init=None,\n+        batch_size=1024,\n+        beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n+        tol=1e-4,\n+        max_no_improvement=10,\n+        max_iter=200,\n+        alpha_W=0.0,\n+        alpha_H=\"same\",\n+        l1_ratio=0.0,\n+        forget_factor=0.7,\n+        fresh_restarts=False,\n+        fresh_restarts_max_iter=30,\n+        transform_max_iter=None,\n+        random_state=None,\n+        verbose=0,\n+    ):\n+\n+        super().__init__(\n+            n_components=n_components,\n+            init=init,\n+            solver=\"mu\",\n+            beta_loss=beta_loss,\n+            tol=tol,\n+            max_iter=max_iter,\n+            random_state=random_state,\n+            alpha_W=alpha_W,\n+            alpha_H=alpha_H,\n+            l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n+            verbose=verbose,\n+        )\n+\n+        self.max_no_improvement = max_no_improvement\n+        self.batch_size = batch_size\n+        self.forget_factor = forget_factor\n+        self.fresh_restarts = fresh_restarts\n+        self.fresh_restarts_max_iter = fresh_restarts_max_iter\n+        self.transform_max_iter = transform_max_iter\n+\n+    def _check_params(self, X):\n+        super()._check_params(X)\n+\n+        # batch_size\n+        self._batch_size = self.batch_size\n+        if not isinstance(self._batch_size, numbers.Integral) or self._batch_size <= 0:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                \"batch_size must be a positive integer, got \"\n+                f\"{self._batch_size!r} instead.\"\n+            )\n+        self._batch_size = min(self._batch_size, X.shape[0])\n+\n+        # forget_factor\n+        self._rho = self.forget_factor ** (self._batch_size / X.shape[0])\n+\n+        # gamma for Maximization-Minimization (MM) algorithm [Fevotte 2011]\n+        if self._beta_loss < 1:\n+            self._gamma = 1.0 / (2.0 - self._beta_loss)\n+        elif self._beta_loss > 2:\n+            self._gamma = 1.0 / (self._beta_loss - 1.0)\n+        else:\n+            self._gamma = 1.0\n+\n+        # transform_max_iter\n+        self._transform_max_iter = (\n+            self.max_iter\n+            if self.transform_max_iter is None\n+            else self.transform_max_iter\n+        )\n+\n+        return self\n+\n+    def _solve_W(self, X, H, max_iter):\n+        \"\"\"Minimize the objective function w.r.t W.\n+\n+        Update W with H being fixed, until convergence. This is the heart\n+        of `transform` but it's also used during `fit` when doing fresh restarts.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        avg = np.sqrt(X.mean() / self._n_components)\n+        W = np.full((X.shape[0], self._n_components), avg, dtype=X.dtype)\n+        W_buffer = W.copy()\n+\n+        # Get scaled regularization terms. Done for each minibatch to take into account\n+        # variable sizes of minibatches.\n+        l1_reg_W, _, l2_reg_W, _ = self._scale_regularization(X)\n+\n+        for _ in range(max_iter):\n+            W, *_ = _multiplicative_update_w(\n+                X, W, H, self._beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, self._gamma\n+            )\n+\n+            W_diff = linalg.norm(W - W_buffer) / linalg.norm(W)\n+            if self.tol > 0 and W_diff <= self.tol:\n+                break\n+\n+            W_buffer[:] = W\n+\n+        return W\n+\n+    def _minibatch_step(self, X, W, H, update_H):\n+        \"\"\"Perform the update of W and H for one minibatch.\"\"\"\n+        batch_size = X.shape[0]\n+\n+        # get scaled regularization terms. Done for each minibatch to take into account\n+        # variable sizes of minibatches.\n+        l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H = self._scale_regularization(X)\n+\n+        # update W\n+        if self.fresh_restarts or W is None:\n+            W = self._solve_W(X, H, self.fresh_restarts_max_iter)\n+        else:\n+            W, *_ = _multiplicative_update_w(\n+                X, W, H, self._beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, self._gamma\n+            )\n+\n+        # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n+        if self._beta_loss < 1:\n+            W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n+\n+        batch_cost = (\n+            _beta_divergence(X, W, H, self._beta_loss)\n+            + l1_reg_W * W.sum()\n+            + l1_reg_H * H.sum()\n+            + l2_reg_W * (W**2).sum()\n+            + l2_reg_H * (H**2).sum()\n+        ) / batch_size\n+\n+        # update H (only at fit or fit_transform)\n+        if update_H:\n+            H[:] = _multiplicative_update_h(\n+                X,\n+                W,\n+                H,\n+                beta_loss=self._beta_loss,\n+                l1_reg_H=l1_reg_H,\n+                l2_reg_H=l2_reg_H,\n+                gamma=self._gamma,\n+                A=self._components_numerator,\n+                B=self._components_denominator,\n+                rho=self._rho,\n+            )\n+\n+            # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n+            if self._beta_loss <= 1:\n+                H[H < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n+\n+        return batch_cost\n+\n+    def _minibatch_convergence(\n+        self, X, batch_cost, H, H_buffer, n_samples, step, n_steps\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"Helper function to encapsulate the early stopping logic\"\"\"\n+        batch_size = X.shape[0]\n+\n+        # counts steps starting from 1 for user friendly verbose mode.\n+        step = step + 1\n+\n+        # Ignore first iteration because H is not updated yet.\n+        if step == 1:\n+            if self.verbose:\n+                print(f\"Minibatch step {step}/{n_steps}: mean batch cost: {batch_cost}\")\n+            return False\n+\n+        # Compute an Exponentially Weighted Average of the cost function to\n+        # monitor the convergence while discarding minibatch-local stochastic\n+        # variability: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moving_average\n+        if self._ewa_cost is None:\n+            self._ewa_cost = batch_cost\n+        else:\n+            alpha = batch_size / (n_samples + 1)\n+            alpha = min(alpha, 1)\n+            self._ewa_cost = self._ewa_cost * (1 - alpha) + batch_cost * alpha\n+\n+        # Log progress to be able to monitor convergence\n+        if self.verbose:\n+            print(\n+                f\"Minibatch step {step}/{n_steps}: mean batch cost: \"\n+                f\"{batch_cost}, ewa cost: {self._ewa_cost}\"\n+            )\n+\n+        # Early stopping based on change of H\n+        H_diff = linalg.norm(H - H_buffer) / linalg.norm(H)\n+        if self.tol > 0 and H_diff <= self.tol:\n+            if self.verbose:\n+                print(f\"Converged (small H change) at step {step}/{n_steps}\")\n+            return True\n+\n+        # Early stopping heuristic due to lack of improvement on smoothed\n+        # cost function\n+        if self._ewa_cost_min is None or self._ewa_cost < self._ewa_cost_min:\n+            self._no_improvement = 0\n+            self._ewa_cost_min = self._ewa_cost\n+        else:\n+            self._no_improvement += 1\n+\n+        if (\n+            self.max_no_improvement is not None\n+            and self._no_improvement >= self.max_no_improvement\n+        ):\n+            if self.verbose:\n+                print(\n+                    \"Converged (lack of improvement in objective function) \"\n+                    f\"at step {step}/{n_steps}\"\n+                )\n+            return True\n+\n+        return False\n+\n+    def fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None):\n+        \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n+\n+        This is more efficient than calling fit followed by transform.\n+\n+        Parameters\n+        ----------\n+        X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n+            Data matrix to be decomposed.\n+\n+        y : Ignored\n+            Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n+\n+        W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n+            If `init='custom'`, it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n+\n+        H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n+            If `init='custom'`, it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n+\n+        Returns\n+        -------\n+        W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n+            Transformed data.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        X = self._validate_data(\n+            X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32]\n+        )\n+\n+        with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n+            W, H, n_iter, n_steps = self._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H)\n+\n+        self.reconstruction_err_ = _beta_divergence(\n+            X, W, H, self._beta_loss, square_root=True\n+        )\n+\n+        self.n_components_ = H.shape[0]\n+        self.components_ = H\n+        self.n_iter_ = n_iter\n+        self.n_steps_ = n_steps\n+\n+        return W\n+\n+    def _fit_transform(self, X, W=None, H=None, update_H=True):\n+        \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n+\n+        Parameters\n+        ----------\n+        X : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n+            Data matrix to be decomposed.\n+\n+        W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n+            If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n+\n+        H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n+            If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n+            If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n+\n+        update_H : bool, default=True\n+            If True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses,\n+            this corresponds to a call to the `fit_transform` method.\n+            If False, only W will be estimated, this corresponds to a call\n+            to the `transform` method.\n+\n+        Returns\n+        -------\n+        W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n+            Transformed data.\n+\n+        H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n+            Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n+\n+        n_iter : int\n+            Actual number of started iterations over the whole dataset.\n+\n+        n_steps : int\n+            Number of mini-batches processed.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        check_non_negative(X, \"NMF (input X)\")\n+        self._check_params(X)\n+\n+        if X.min() == 0 and self._beta_loss <= 0:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                \"When beta_loss <= 0 and X contains zeros, \"\n+                \"the solver may diverge. Please add small values \"\n+                \"to X, or use a positive beta_loss.\"\n+            )\n+\n+        n_samples = X.shape[0]\n+\n+        # initialize or check W and H\n+        W, H = self._check_w_h(X, W, H, update_H)\n+        H_buffer = H.copy()\n+\n+        # Initialize auxiliary matrices\n+        self._components_numerator = H.copy()\n+        self._components_denominator = np.ones(H.shape, dtype=H.dtype)\n+\n+        # Attributes to monitor the convergence\n+        self._ewa_cost = None\n+        self._ewa_cost_min = None\n+        self._no_improvement = 0\n+\n+        batches = gen_batches(n_samples, self._batch_size)\n+        batches = itertools.cycle(batches)\n+        n_steps_per_iter = int(np.ceil(n_samples / self._batch_size))\n+        n_steps = self.max_iter * n_steps_per_iter\n+\n+        for i, batch in zip(range(n_steps), batches):\n+\n+            batch_cost = self._minibatch_step(X[batch], W[batch], H, update_H)\n+\n+            if update_H and self._minibatch_convergence(\n+                X[batch], batch_cost, H, H_buffer, n_samples, i, n_steps\n+            ):\n+                break\n+\n+            H_buffer[:] = H\n+\n+        if self.fresh_restarts:\n+            W = self._solve_W(X, H, self._transform_max_iter)\n+\n+        n_steps = i + 1\n+        n_iter = int(np.ceil(n_steps / n_steps_per_iter))\n+\n+        if n_iter == self.max_iter and self.tol > 0:\n+            warnings.warn(\n+                f\"Maximum number of iterations {self.max_iter} reached. \"\n+                \"Increase it to improve convergence.\",\n+                ConvergenceWarning,\n+            )\n+\n+        return W, H, n_iter, n_steps\n+\n+    def transform(self, X):\n+        \"\"\"Transform the data X according to the fitted MiniBatchNMF model.\n+\n+        Parameters\n+        ----------\n+        X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n+            Data matrix to be transformed by the model.\n+\n+        Returns\n+        -------\n+        W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n+            Transformed data.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        check_is_fitted(self)\n+        X = self._validate_data(\n+            X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32], reset=False\n+        )\n+\n+        W = self._solve_W(X, self.components_, self._transform_max_iter)\n+\n+        return W\n+\n+    def partial_fit(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None):\n+        \"\"\"Update the model using the data in `X` as a mini-batch.\n+\n+        This method is expected to be called several times consecutively\n+        on different chunks of a dataset so as to implement out-of-core\n+        or online learning.\n+\n+        This is especially useful when the whole dataset is too big to fit in\n+        memory at once (see :ref:`scaling_strategies`).\n+\n+        Parameters\n+        ----------\n+        X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n+            Data matrix to be decomposed.\n+\n+        y : Ignored\n+            Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n+\n+        W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n+            If `init='custom'`, it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n+            Only used for the first call to `partial_fit`.\n+\n+        H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n+            If `init='custom'`, it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n+            Only used for the first call to `partial_fit`.\n+\n+        Returns\n+        -------\n+        self\n+            Returns the instance itself.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        has_components = hasattr(self, \"components_\")\n+\n+        X = self._validate_data(\n+            X,\n+            accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"),\n+            dtype=[np.float64, np.float32],\n+            reset=not has_components,\n+        )\n+\n+        if not has_components:\n+            # This instance has not been fitted yet (fit or partial_fit)\n+            self._check_params(X)\n+            _, H = self._check_w_h(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=True)\n+\n+            self._components_numerator = H.copy()\n+            self._components_denominator = np.ones(H.shape, dtype=H.dtype)\n+            self.n_steps_ = 0\n+        else:\n+            H = self.components_\n+\n+        self._minibatch_step(X, None, H, update_H=True)\n+\n+        self.n_components_ = H.shape[0]\n+        self.components_ = H\n+        self.n_steps_ += 1\n+\n+        return self\ndiff --git a/sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py b/sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nindex 5e9601ae220a6..cc51507f90d49 100644\n--- a/sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\n+++ b/sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\n@@ -652,6 +652,9 @@ def _set_checking_parameters(estimator):\n         # NMF\n         if name == \"NMF\":\n             estimator.set_params(max_iter=500)\n+        # MiniBatchNMF\n+        if estimator.__class__.__name__ == \"MiniBatchNMF\":\n+            estimator.set_params(max_iter=20, fresh_restarts=True)\n         # MLP\n         if name in [\"MLPClassifier\", \"MLPRegressor\"]:\n             estimator.set_params(max_iter=100)\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 60064, "completion_tokens": 350}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: Online implementation of Non-negative Matrix Factorizarion (NMF)\n\n<!--\r\nIf your issue is a usage question, submit it here instead:\r\n- StackOverflow with the scikit-learn tag: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/scikit-learn\r\n- Mailing List: https://mail.python.org/mailman/listinfo/scikit-learn\r\nFor more information, see User Questions: http://scikit-learn.org/stable/support.html#user-questions\r\n-->\r\n\r\n<!-- Instructions For Filing a Bug: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#filing-bugs -->\r\n\r\n#### Description\r\n<!-- Example: Joblib Error thrown when calling fit on LatentDirichletAllocation with evaluate_every > 0-->\r\n@GaelVaroquaux Maybe an online version of the NMF could be useful in big data settings.\r\n\r\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\r\n<!--\r\nExample:\r\n```python\r\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\r\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import LatentDirichletAllocation\r\n\r\ndocs = [\"Help I have a bug\" for i in range(1000)]\r\n\r\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(input=docs, analyzer='word')\r\nlda_features = vectorizer.fit_transform(docs)\r\n\r\nlda_model = LatentDirichletAllocation(\r\n    n_topics=10,\r\n    learning_method='online',\r\n    evaluate_every=10,\r\n    n_jobs=4,\r\n)\r\nmodel = lda_model.fit(lda_features)\r\n```\r\nIf the code is too long, feel free to put it in a public gist and link\r\nit in the issue: https://gist.github.com\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Expected Results\r\n<!-- Example: No error is thrown. Please paste or describe the expected results.-->\r\n\r\n#### Actual Results\r\n<!-- Please paste or specifically describe the actual output or traceback. -->\r\nHere I show the results for an sparse matrix as input of size 1M x 2.8k\r\nThe factorization has `n_components = 10` and each point is an entire pass on the data.\r\nThe current online implementation only supports the kullback-leibler divergence, but in practice\r\ncan be generalized to any beta divergence.\r\n\r\n![batch_vs_online_nmf](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11759948/53494592-69748b00-3a9e-11e9-8cc8-ce53ce3fe7f2.png)\r\n\r\n#### Versions\r\n<!--\r\nPlease run the following snippet and paste the output below.\r\nFor scikit-learn >= 0.20:\r\nimport sklearn; sklearn.show_versions()\r\nFor scikit-learn < 0.20:\r\nimport platform; print(platform.platform())\r\nimport sys; print(\"Python\", sys.version)\r\nimport numpy; print(\"NumPy\", numpy.__version__)\r\nimport scipy; print(\"SciPy\", scipy.__version__)\r\nimport sklearn; print(\"Scikit-Learn\", sklearn.__version__)\r\n-->\r\n\r\n\r\n<!-- Thanks for contributing! -->\r\n\nDraft implementation of MiniBatchNMF\n<!--\r\nThanks for contributing a pull request! Please ensure you have taken a look at\r\nthe contribution guidelines: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#pull-request-checklist\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Reference Issues/PRs\r\n<!--\r\nExample: Fixes #1234. See also #3456.\r\nPlease use keywords (e.g., Fixes) to create link to the issues or pull requests\r\nyou resolved, so that they will automatically be closed when your pull request\r\nis merged. See https://github.com/blog/1506-closing-issues-via-pull-requests\r\n-->\r\n\r\n\r\n#### What does this implement/fix? Explain your changes.\r\n\r\nImplementation of a mini-batch NMF with multiplicative updates.\r\n#13308 \r\n#### Any other comments?\r\n\r\n\r\n<!--\r\nPlease be aware that we are a loose team of volunteers so patience is\r\nnecessary; assistance handling other issues is very welcome. We value\r\nall user contributions, no matter how minor they are. If we are slow to\r\nreview, either the pull request needs some benchmarking, tinkering,\r\nconvincing, etc. or more likely the reviewers are simply busy. In either\r\ncase, we ask for your understanding during the review process.\r\nFor more information, see our FAQ on this topic:\r\nhttp://scikit-learn.org/dev/faq.html#why-is-my-pull-request-not-getting-any-attention.\r\n\r\nThanks for contributing!\r\n-->\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental evidence from software dynamic analysis is available through a tool for this instance: `get_trace_artifacts`. \nThis tool provides compact raw slices of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts, more specifically:\n- `get_trace_artifacts`: The unique execution trace artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis. \n    This analysis involves executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled, \n    tracing their execution, and subtracting the overlapping parts from execution trace of A.py. \n    The tool returns the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nSuggested workflow:\n- At the beginning of analyzing a new data point, first inspect the raw execution-trace evidence with `get_trace_artifacts`.\n- During the analysis, you may call `get_trace_artifacts` again with narrower filters such as `path_contains` or `name_contains`.\n- You can revisit this tool at any point in the investigation; they are not only for the beginning.\n- Treat these tool outputs as compact raw evidence and verify them with the regular repository tools before finishing.\n\nNote: \n- Use the supplemental evidence to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself."}, {"content": "Let's start by inspecting the execution-trace artifacts to identify potential files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue of adding an online implementation of Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n\n<function=get_trace_artifacts>\n<parameter=artifact_type>all</parameter>\n<parameter=path_contains>decomposition</parameter>\n<parameter=name_contains>nmf</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_trace_artifacts", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n{\n  \"instance_id\": \"scikit-learn__scikit-learn-16948\",\n  \"trace_summary\": {\n    \"unique_function_count\": 110,\n    \"returned_count\": 8,\n    \"filtered_count\": 12\n  },\n  \"applied_filters\": {\n    \"artifact_type\": \"all\",\n    \"path_contains\": \"decomposition\",\n    \"name_contains\": \"nmf\",\n    \"start\": 0,\n    \"limit\": 8\n  },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"_initialize_nmf\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": 235,\n      \"end_line\": 387,\n      \"event_count\": 38,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"NMF.__init__\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1385,\n      \"end_line\": 1416,\n      \"event_count\": 16,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"NMF._check_params\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1421,\n      \"end_line\": 1511,\n      \"event_count\": 28,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"NMF._check_w_h\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1513,\n      \"end_line\": 1542,\n      \"event_count\": 9,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"NMF._scale_regularization\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1544,\n      \"end_line\": 1561,\n      \"event_count\": 18000,\n      \"call_count\": 2000,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"NMF.fit\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1701,\n      \"end_line\": 1723,\n      \"event_count\": 4,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"MiniBatchNMF.__init__\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1973,\n      \"end_line\": 2013,\n      \"event_count\": 21,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"MiniBatchNMF._check_params\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 2015,\n      \"end_line\": 2045,\n      \"event_count\": 14,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 4\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_more\": true,\n  \"next_start\": 8\n}"}, {"content": "Based on the initial execution-trace artifacts, it seems that the `_nmf.py` file is central to the implementation of NMF and MiniBatchNMF. We'll focus on this file for further analysis.\n\nLet's get the full content of `_nmf.py` to inspect the relevant classes and methods.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py`.\n```\n   1 | \"\"\" Non-negative matrix factorization.\n   2 | \"\"\"\n   3 | # Author: Vlad Niculae\n   4 | #         Lars Buitinck\n   5 | #         Mathieu Blondel <mathieu@mblondel.org>\n   6 | #         Tom Dupre la Tour\n   7 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n   8 | \n   9 | import numbers\n  10 | import numpy as np\n  11 | import scipy.sparse as sp\n  12 | import time\n  13 | import warnings\n  14 | from math import sqrt\n  15 | \n  16 | from ._cdnmf_fast import _update_cdnmf_fast\n  17 | from .._config import config_context\n  18 | from ..base import BaseEstimator, TransformerMixin, _ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin\n  19 | from ..exceptions import ConvergenceWarning\n  20 | from ..utils import check_random_state, check_array\n  21 | from ..utils.extmath import randomized_svd, safe_sparse_dot, squared_norm\n  22 | from ..utils.validation import (\n  23 |     check_is_fitted,\n  24 |     check_non_negative,\n  25 | )\n  26 | \n  27 | EPSILON = np.finfo(np.float32).eps\n  28 | \n  29 | \n  30 | def norm(x):\n  31 |     \"\"\"Dot product-based Euclidean norm implementation.\n  32 | \n  33 |     See: http://fseoane.net/blog/2011/computing-the-vector-norm/\n  34 | \n  35 |     Parameters\n  36 |     ----------\n  37 |     x : array-like\n  38 |         Vector for which to compute the norm.\n  39 |     \"\"\"\n  40 |     return sqrt(squared_norm(x))\n  41 | \n  42 | \n  43 | def trace_dot(X, Y):\n  44 |     \"\"\"Trace of np.dot(X, Y.T).\n  45 | \n  46 |     Parameters\n  47 |     ----------\n  48 |     X : array-like\n  49 |         First matrix.\n  50 |     Y : array-like\n  51 |         Second matrix.\n  52 |     \"\"\"\n  53 |     return np.dot(X.ravel(), Y.ravel())\n  54 | \n  55 | \n  56 | def _check_init(A, shape, whom):\n  57 |     A = check_array(A)\n  58 |     if np.shape(A) != shape:\n  59 |         raise ValueError(\n  60 |             \"Array with wrong shape passed to %s. Expected %s, but got %s \"\n  61 |             % (whom, shape, np.shape(A))\n  62 |         )\n  63 |     check_non_negative(A, whom)\n  64 |     if np.max(A) == 0:\n  65 |         raise ValueError(\"Array passed to %s is full of zeros.\" % whom)\n  66 | \n  67 | \n  68 | def _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta, square_root=False):\n  69 |     \"\"\"Compute the beta-divergence of X and dot(W, H).\n  70 | \n  71 |     Parameters\n  72 |     ----------\n  73 |     X : float or array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n  74 | \n  75 |     W : float or array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n  76 | \n  77 |     H : float or array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n  78 | \n  79 |     beta : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}\n  80 |         Parameter of the beta-divergence.\n  81 |         If beta == 2, this is half the Frobenius *squared* norm.\n  82 |         If beta == 1, this is the generalized Kullback-Leibler divergence.\n  83 |         If beta == 0, this is the Itakura-Saito divergence.\n  84 |         Else, this is the general beta-divergence.\n  85 | \n  86 |     square_root : bool, default=False\n  87 |         If True, return np.sqrt(2 * res)\n  88 |         For beta == 2, it corresponds to the Frobenius norm.\n  89 | \n  90 |     Returns\n  91 |     -------\n  92 |         res : float\n  93 |             Beta divergence of X and np.dot(X, H).\n  94 |     \"\"\"\n  95 |     beta = _beta_loss_to_float(beta)\n  96 | \n  97 |     # The method can be called with scalars\n  98 |     if not sp.issparse(X):\n  99 |         X = np.atleast_2d(X)\n 100 |     W = np.atleast_2d(W)\n 101 |     H = np.atleast_2d(H)\n 102 | \n 103 |     # Frobenius norm\n 104 |     if beta == 2:\n 105 |         # Avoid the creation of the dense np.dot(W, H) if X is sparse.\n 106 |         if sp.issparse(X):\n 107 |             norm_X = np.dot(X.data, X.data)\n 108 |             norm_WH = trace_dot(np.linalg.multi_dot([W.T, W, H]), H)\n 109 |             cross_prod = trace_dot((X * H.T), W)\n 110 |             res = (norm_X + norm_WH - 2.0 * cross_prod) / 2.0\n 111 |         else:\n 112 |             res = squared_norm(X - np.dot(W, H)) / 2.0\n 113 | \n 114 |         if square_root:\n 115 |             return np.sqrt(res * 2)\n 116 |         else:\n 117 |             return res\n 118 | \n 119 |     if sp.issparse(X):\n 120 |         # compute np.dot(W, H) only where X is nonzero\n 121 |         WH_data = _special_sparse_dot(W, H, X).data\n 122 |         X_data = X.data\n 123 |     else:\n 124 |         WH = np.dot(W, H)\n 125 |         WH_data = WH.ravel()\n 126 |         X_data = X.ravel()\n 127 | \n 128 |     # do not affect the zeros: here 0 ** (-1) = 0 and not infinity\n 129 |     indices = X_data > EPSILON\n 130 |     WH_data = WH_data[indices]\n 131 |     X_data = X_data[indices]\n 132 | \n 133 |     # used to avoid division by zero\n 134 |     WH_data[WH_data == 0] = EPSILON\n 135 | \n 136 |     # generalized Kullback-Leibler divergence\n 137 |     if beta == 1:\n 138 |         # fast and memory efficient computation of np.sum(np.dot(W, H))\n 139 |         sum_WH = np.dot(np.sum(W, axis=0), np.sum(H, axis=1))\n 140 |         # computes np.sum(X * log(X / WH)) only where X is nonzero\n 141 |         div = X_data / WH_data\n 142 |         res = np.dot(X_data, np.log(div))\n 143 |         # add full np.sum(np.dot(W, H)) - np.sum(X)\n 144 |         res += sum_WH - X_data.sum()\n 145 | \n 146 |     # Itakura-Saito divergence\n 147 |     elif beta == 0:\n 148 |         div = X_data / WH_data\n 149 |         res = np.sum(div) - np.product(X.shape) - np.sum(np.log(div))\n 150 | \n 151 |     # beta-divergence, beta not in (0, 1, 2)\n 152 |     else:\n 153 |         if sp.issparse(X):\n 154 |             # slow loop, but memory efficient computation of :\n 155 |             # np.sum(np.dot(W, H) ** beta)\n 156 |             sum_WH_beta = 0\n 157 |             for i in range(X.shape[1]):\n 158 |                 sum_WH_beta += np.sum(np.dot(W, H[:, i]) ** beta)\n 159 | \n 160 |         else:\n 161 |             sum_WH_beta = np.sum(WH**beta)\n 162 | \n 163 |         sum_X_WH = np.dot(X_data, WH_data ** (beta - 1))\n 164 |         res = (X_data**beta).sum() - beta * sum_X_WH\n 165 |         res += sum_WH_beta * (beta - 1)\n 166 |         res /= beta * (beta - 1)\n 167 | \n 168 |     if square_root:\n 169 |         return np.sqrt(2 * res)\n 170 |     else:\n 171 |         return res\n 172 | \n 173 | \n 174 | def _special_sparse_dot(W, H, X):\n 175 |     \"\"\"Computes np.dot(W, H), only where X is non zero.\"\"\"\n 176 |     if sp.issparse(X):\n 177 |         ii, jj = X.nonzero()\n 178 |         n_vals = ii.shape[0]\n 179 |         dot_vals = np.empty(n_vals)\n 180 |         n_components = W.shape[1]\n 181 | \n 182 |         batch_size = max(n_components, n_vals // n_components)\n 183 |         for start in range(0, n_vals, batch_size):\n 184 |             batch = slice(start, start + batch_size)\n 185 |             dot_vals[batch] = np.multiply(W[ii[batch], :], H.T[jj[batch], :]).sum(\n 186 |                 axis=1\n 187 |             )\n 188 | \n 189 |         WH = sp.coo_matrix((dot_vals, (ii, jj)), shape=X.shape)\n 190 |         return WH.tocsr()\n 191 |     else:\n 192 |         return np.dot(W, H)\n 193 | \n 194 | \n 195 | def _compute_regularization(alpha, alpha_W, alpha_H, l1_ratio, regularization):\n 196 |     \"\"\"Compute L1 and L2 regularization coefficients for W and H.\"\"\"\n 197 |     if alpha_W != 0 or alpha_H != \"same\":\n 198 |         # if alpha_W or alpha_H is not left to its default value we ignore alpha and\n 199 |         # regularization.\n 200 |         alpha_H = alpha_W if alpha_H == \"same\" else alpha_H\n 201 |         l1_reg_W = alpha_W * l1_ratio\n 202 |         l1_reg_H = alpha_H * l1_ratio\n 203 |         l2_reg_W = alpha_W * (1.0 - l1_ratio)\n 204 |         l2_reg_H = alpha_H * (1.0 - l1_ratio)\n 205 |     else:\n 206 |         # TODO remove in 1.2\n 207 |         l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H = 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0\n 208 |         if regularization in (\"both\", \"transformation\"):\n 209 |             l1_reg_W = alpha * l1_ratio\n 210 |             l2_reg_W = alpha * (1.0 - l1_ratio)\n 211 |         if regularization in (\"both\", \"components\"):\n 212 |             l1_reg_H = alpha * l1_ratio\n 213 |             l2_reg_H = alpha * (1.0 - l1_ratio)\n 214 | \n 215 |     return l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H\n 216 | \n 217 | \n 218 | def _beta_loss_to_float(beta_loss):\n 219 |     \"\"\"Convert string beta_loss to float.\"\"\"\n 220 |     allowed_beta_loss = {\"frobenius\": 2, \"kullback-leibler\": 1, \"itakura-saito\": 0}\n 221 |     if isinstance(beta_loss, str) and beta_loss in allowed_beta_loss:\n 222 |         beta_loss = allowed_beta_loss[beta_loss]\n 223 | \n 224 |     if not isinstance(beta_loss, numbers.Number):\n 225 |         raise ValueError(\n 226 |             \"Invalid beta_loss parameter: got %r instead of one of %r, or a float.\"\n 227 |             % (beta_loss, allowed_beta_loss.keys())\n 228 |         )\n 229 |     return beta_loss\n 230 | \n 231 | \n 232 | def _initialize_nmf(X, n_components, init=None, eps=1e-6, random_state=None):\n 233 |     \"\"\"Algorithms for NMF initialization.\n 234 | \n 235 |     Computes an initial guess for the non-negative\n 236 |     rank k matrix approximation for X: X = WH.\n 237 | \n 238 |     Parameters\n 239 |     ----------\n 240 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 241 |         The data matrix to be decomposed.\n 242 | \n 243 |     n_components : int\n 244 |         The number of components desired in the approximation.\n 245 | \n 246 |     init :  {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar'}, default=None\n 247 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 248 |         Valid options:\n 249 | \n 250 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features),\n 251 |             otherwise 'random'.\n 252 | \n 253 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 254 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 255 | \n 256 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 257 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 258 | \n 259 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 260 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 261 | \n 262 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 263 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 264 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 265 | \n 266 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H\n 267 | \n 268 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 269 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 270 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 271 | \n 272 |     eps : float, default=1e-6\n 273 |         Truncate all values less then this in output to zero.\n 274 | \n 275 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n 276 |         Used when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or 'random'. Pass an int for\n 277 |         reproducible results across multiple function calls.\n 278 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 279 | \n 280 |     Returns\n 281 |     -------\n 282 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 283 |         Initial guesses for solving X ~= WH.\n 284 | \n 285 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n 286 |         Initial guesses for solving X ~= WH.\n 287 | \n 288 |     References\n 289 |     ----------\n 290 |     C. Boutsidis, E. Gallopoulos: SVD based initialization: A head start for\n 291 |     nonnegative matrix factorization - Pattern Recognition, 2008\n 292 |     http://tinyurl.com/nndsvd\n 293 |     \"\"\"\n 294 |     check_non_negative(X, \"NMF initialization\")\n 295 |     n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n 296 | \n 297 |     if (\n 298 |         init is not None\n 299 |         and init != \"random\"\n 300 |         and n_components > min(n_samples, n_features)\n 301 |     ):\n 302 |         raise ValueError(\n 303 |             \"init = '{}' can only be used when \"\n 304 |             \"n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features)\".format(init)\n 305 |         )\n 306 | \n 307 |     if init is None:\n 308 |         if n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features):\n 309 |             init = \"nndsvda\"\n 310 |         else:\n 311 |             init = \"random\"\n 312 | \n 313 |     # Random initialization\n 314 |     if init == \"random\":\n 315 |         avg = np.sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 316 |         rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n 317 |         H = avg * rng.standard_normal(size=(n_components, n_features)).astype(\n 318 |             X.dtype, copy=False\n 319 |         )\n 320 |         W = avg * rng.standard_normal(size=(n_samples, n_components)).astype(\n 321 |             X.dtype, copy=False\n 322 |         )\n 323 |         np.abs(H, out=H)\n 324 |         np.abs(W, out=W)\n 325 |         return W, H\n 326 | \n 327 |     # NNDSVD initialization\n 328 |     U, S, V = randomized_svd(X, n_components, random_state=random_state)\n 329 |     W = np.zeros_like(U)\n 330 |     H = np.zeros_like(V)\n 331 | \n 332 |     # The leading singular triplet is non-negative\n 333 |     # so it can be used as is for initialization.\n 334 |     W[:, 0] = np.sqrt(S[0]) * np.abs(U[:, 0])\n 335 |     H[0, :] = np.sqrt(S[0]) * np.abs(V[0, :])\n 336 | \n 337 |     for j in range(1, n_components):\n 338 |         x, y = U[:, j], V[j, :]\n 339 | \n 340 |         # extract positive and negative parts of column vectors\n 341 |         x_p, y_p = np.maximum(x, 0), np.maximum(y, 0)\n 342 |         x_n, y_n = np.abs(np.minimum(x, 0)), np.abs(np.minimum(y, 0))\n 343 | \n 344 |         # and their norms\n 345 |         x_p_nrm, y_p_nrm = norm(x_p), norm(y_p)\n 346 |         x_n_nrm, y_n_nrm = norm(x_n), norm(y_n)\n 347 | \n 348 |         m_p, m_n = x_p_nrm * y_p_nrm, x_n_nrm * y_n_nrm\n 349 | \n 350 |         # choose update\n 351 |         if m_p > m_n:\n 352 |             u = x_p / x_p_nrm\n 353 |             v = y_p / y_p_nrm\n 354 |             sigma = m_p\n 355 |         else:\n 356 |             u = x_n / x_n_nrm\n 357 |             v = y_n / y_n_nrm\n 358 |             sigma = m_n\n 359 | \n 360 |         lbd = np.sqrt(S[j] * sigma)\n 361 |         W[:, j] = lbd * u\n 362 |         H[j, :] = lbd * v\n 363 | \n 364 |     W[W < eps] = 0\n 365 |     H[H < eps] = 0\n 366 | \n 367 |     if init == \"nndsvd\":\n 368 |         pass\n 369 |     elif init == \"nndsvda\":\n 370 |         avg = X.mean()\n 371 |         W[W == 0] = avg\n 372 |         H[H == 0] = avg\n 373 |     elif init == \"nndsvdar\":\n 374 |         rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n 375 |         avg = X.mean()\n 376 |         W[W == 0] = abs(avg * rng.standard_normal(size=len(W[W == 0])) / 100)\n 377 |         H[H == 0] = abs(avg * rng.standard_normal(size=len(H[H == 0])) / 100)\n 378 |     else:\n 379 |         raise ValueError(\n 380 |             \"Invalid init parameter: got %r instead of one of %r\"\n 381 |             % (init, (None, \"random\", \"nndsvd\", \"nndsvda\", \"nndsvdar\"))\n 382 |         )\n 383 | \n 384 |     return W, H\n 385 | \n 386 | \n 387 | def _update_coordinate_descent(X, W, Ht, l1_reg, l2_reg, shuffle, random_state):\n 388 |     \"\"\"Helper function for _fit_coordinate_descent.\n 389 | \n 390 |     Update W to minimize the objective function, iterating once over all\n 391 |     coordinates. By symmetry, to update H, one can call\n 392 |     _update_coordinate_descent(X.T, Ht, W, ...).\n 393 | \n 394 |     \"\"\"\n 395 |     n_components = Ht.shape[1]\n 396 | \n 397 |     HHt = np.dot(Ht.T, Ht)\n 398 |     XHt = safe_sparse_dot(X, Ht)\n 399 | \n 400 |     # L2 regularization corresponds to increase of the diagonal of HHt\n 401 |     if l2_reg != 0.0:\n 402 |         # adds l2_reg only on the diagonal\n 403 |         HHt.flat[:: n_components + 1] += l2_reg\n 404 |     # L1 regularization corresponds to decrease of each element of XHt\n 405 |     if l1_reg != 0.0:\n 406 |         XHt -= l1_reg\n 407 | \n 408 |     if shuffle:\n 409 |         permutation = random_state.permutation(n_components)\n 410 |     else:\n 411 |         permutation = np.arange(n_components)\n 412 |     # The following seems to be required on 64-bit Windows w/ Python 3.5.\n 413 |     permutation = np.asarray(permutation, dtype=np.intp)\n 414 |     return _update_cdnmf_fast(W, HHt, XHt, permutation)\n 415 | \n 416 | \n 417 | def _fit_coordinate_descent(\n 418 |     X,\n 419 |     W,\n 420 |     H,\n 421 |     tol=1e-4,\n 422 |     max_iter=200,\n 423 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n 424 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n 425 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n 426 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n 427 |     update_H=True,\n 428 |     verbose=0,\n 429 |     shuffle=False,\n 430 |     random_state=None,\n 431 | ):\n 432 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) with Coordinate Descent\n 433 | \n 434 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 435 |     and H. Each minimization is done with a cyclic (up to a permutation of the\n 436 |     features) Coordinate Descent.\n 437 | \n 438 |     Parameters\n 439 |     ----------\n 440 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 441 |         Constant matrix.\n 442 | \n 443 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 444 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n 445 | \n 446 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n 447 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n 448 | \n 449 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n 450 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n 451 | \n 452 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n 453 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n 454 | \n 455 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n 456 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n 457 | \n 458 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n 459 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n 460 | \n 461 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n 462 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n 463 | \n 464 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n 465 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n 466 | \n 467 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 468 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 469 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 470 | \n 471 |     verbose : int, default=0\n 472 |         The verbosity level.\n 473 | \n 474 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n 475 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n 476 | \n 477 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n 478 |         Used to randomize the coordinates in the CD solver, when\n 479 |         ``shuffle`` is set to ``True``. Pass an int for reproducible\n 480 |         results across multiple function calls.\n 481 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 482 | \n 483 |     Returns\n 484 |     -------\n 485 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 486 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 487 | \n 488 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n 489 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 490 | \n 491 |     n_iter : int\n 492 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n 493 | \n 494 |     References\n 495 |     ----------\n 496 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and Phan, Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n 497 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n 498 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n 499 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n 500 |     \"\"\"\n 501 |     # so W and Ht are both in C order in memory\n 502 |     Ht = check_array(H.T, order=\"C\")\n 503 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=\"csr\")\n 504 | \n 505 |     rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n 506 | \n 507 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n 508 |         violation = 0.0\n 509 | \n 510 |         # Update W\n 511 |         violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n 512 |             X, W, Ht, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, shuffle, rng\n 513 |         )\n 514 |         # Update H\n 515 |         if update_H:\n 516 |             violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n 517 |                 X.T, Ht, W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, shuffle, rng\n 518 |             )\n 519 | \n 520 |         if n_iter == 1:\n 521 |             violation_init = violation\n 522 | \n 523 |         if violation_init == 0:\n 524 |             break\n 525 | \n 526 |         if verbose:\n 527 |             print(\"violation:\", violation / violation_init)\n 528 | \n 529 |         if violation / violation_init <= tol:\n 530 |             if verbose:\n 531 |                 print(\"Converged at iteration\", n_iter + 1)\n 532 |             break\n 533 | \n 534 |     return W, Ht.T, n_iter\n 535 | \n 536 | \n 537 | def _multiplicative_update_w(\n 538 |     X,\n 539 |     W,\n 540 |     H,\n 541 |     beta_loss,\n 542 |     l1_reg_W,\n 543 |     l2_reg_W,\n 544 |     gamma,\n 545 |     H_sum=None,\n 546 |     HHt=None,\n 547 |     XHt=None,\n 548 |     update_H=True,\n 549 | ):\n 550 |     \"\"\"Update W in Multiplicative Update NMF.\"\"\"\n 551 |     if beta_loss == 2:\n 552 |         # Numerator\n 553 |         if XHt is None:\n 554 |             XHt = safe_sparse_dot(X, H.T)\n 555 |         if update_H:\n 556 |             # avoid a copy of XHt, which will be re-computed (update_H=True)\n 557 |             numerator = XHt\n 558 |         else:\n 559 |             # preserve the XHt, which is not re-computed (update_H=False)\n 560 |             numerator = XHt.copy()\n 561 | \n 562 |         # Denominator\n 563 |         if HHt is None:\n 564 |             HHt = np.dot(H, H.T)\n 565 |         denominator = np.dot(W, HHt)\n 566 | \n 567 |     else:\n 568 |         # Numerator\n 569 |         # if X is sparse, compute WH only where X is non zero\n 570 |         WH_safe_X = _special_sparse_dot(W, H, X)\n 571 |         if sp.issparse(X):\n 572 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X.data\n 573 |             X_data = X.data\n 574 |         else:\n 575 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X\n 576 |             X_data = X\n 577 |             # copy used in the Denominator\n 578 |             WH = WH_safe_X.copy()\n 579 |             if beta_loss - 1.0 < 0:\n 580 |                 WH[WH == 0] = EPSILON\n 581 | \n 582 |         # to avoid taking a negative power of zero\n 583 |         if beta_loss - 2.0 < 0:\n 584 |             WH_safe_X_data[WH_safe_X_data == 0] = EPSILON\n 585 | \n 586 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n 587 |             np.divide(X_data, WH_safe_X_data, out=WH_safe_X_data)\n 588 |         elif beta_loss == 0:\n 589 |             # speeds up computation time\n 590 |             # refer to /numpy/numpy/issues/9363\n 591 |             WH_safe_X_data **= -1\n 592 |             WH_safe_X_data **= 2\n 593 |             # element-wise multiplication\n 594 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n 595 |         else:\n 596 |             WH_safe_X_data **= beta_loss - 2\n 597 |             # element-wise multiplication\n 598 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n 599 | \n 600 |         # here numerator = dot(X * (dot(W, H) ** (beta_loss - 2)), H.T)\n 601 |         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(WH_safe_X, H.T)\n 602 | \n 603 |         # Denominator\n 604 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n 605 |             if H_sum is None:\n 606 |                 H_sum = np.sum(H, axis=1)  # shape(n_components, )\n 607 |             denominator = H_sum[np.newaxis, :]\n 608 | \n 609 |         else:\n 610 |             # computation of WHHt = dot(dot(W, H) ** beta_loss - 1, H.T)\n 611 |             if sp.issparse(X):\n 612 |                 # memory efficient computation\n 613 |                 # (compute row by row, avoiding the dense matrix WH)\n 614 |                 WHHt = np.empty(W.shape)\n 615 |                 for i in range(X.shape[0]):\n 616 |                     WHi = np.dot(W[i, :], H)\n 617 |                     if beta_loss - 1 < 0:\n 618 |                         WHi[WHi == 0] = EPSILON\n 619 |                     WHi **= beta_loss - 1\n 620 |                     WHHt[i, :] = np.dot(WHi, H.T)\n 621 |             else:\n 622 |                 WH **= beta_loss - 1\n 623 |                 WHHt = np.dot(WH, H.T)\n 624 |             denominator = WHHt\n 625 | \n 626 |     # Add L1 and L2 regularization\n 627 |     if l1_reg_W > 0:\n 628 |         denominator += l1_reg_W\n 629 |     if l2_reg_W > 0:\n 630 |         denominator = denominator + l2_reg_W * W\n 631 |     denominator[denominator == 0] = EPSILON\n 632 | \n 633 |     numerator /= denominator\n 634 |     delta_W = numerator\n 635 | \n 636 |     # gamma is in ]0, 1]\n 637 |     if gamma != 1:\n 638 |         delta_W **= gamma\n 639 | \n 640 |     return delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt\n 641 | \n 642 | \n 643 | def _multiplicative_update_h(X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma):\n 644 |     \"\"\"Update H in Multiplicative Update NMF.\"\"\"\n 645 |     if beta_loss == 2:\n 646 |         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, X)\n 647 |         denominator = np.linalg.multi_dot([W.T, W, H])\n 648 | \n 649 |     else:\n 650 |         # Numerator\n 651 |         WH_safe_X = _special_sparse_dot(W, H, X)\n 652 |         if sp.issparse(X):\n 653 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X.data\n 654 |             X_data = X.data\n 655 |         else:\n 656 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X\n 657 |             X_data = X\n 658 |             # copy used in the Denominator\n 659 |             WH = WH_safe_X.copy()\n 660 |             if beta_loss - 1.0 < 0:\n 661 |                 WH[WH == 0] = EPSILON\n 662 | \n 663 |         # to avoid division by zero\n 664 |         if beta_loss - 2.0 < 0:\n 665 |             WH_safe_X_data[WH_safe_X_data == 0] = EPSILON\n 666 | \n 667 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n 668 |             np.divide(X_data, WH_safe_X_data, out=WH_safe_X_data)\n 669 |         elif beta_loss == 0:\n 670 |             # speeds up computation time\n 671 |             # refer to /numpy/numpy/issues/9363\n 672 |             WH_safe_X_data **= -1\n 673 |             WH_safe_X_data **= 2\n 674 |             # element-wise multiplication\n 675 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n 676 |         else:\n 677 |             WH_safe_X_data **= beta_loss - 2\n 678 |             # element-wise multiplication\n 679 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n 680 | \n 681 |         # here numerator = dot(W.T, (dot(W, H) ** (beta_loss - 2)) * X)\n 682 |         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, WH_safe_X)\n 683 | \n 684 |         # Denominator\n 685 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n 686 |             W_sum = np.sum(W, axis=0)  # shape(n_components, )\n 687 |             W_sum[W_sum == 0] = 1.0\n 688 |             denominator = W_sum[:, np.newaxis]\n 689 | \n 690 |         # beta_loss not in (1, 2)\n 691 |         else:\n 692 |             # computation of WtWH = dot(W.T, dot(W, H) ** beta_loss - 1)\n 693 |             if sp.issparse(X):\n 694 |                 # memory efficient computation\n 695 |                 # (compute column by column, avoiding the dense matrix WH)\n 696 |                 WtWH = np.empty(H.shape)\n 697 |                 for i in range(X.shape[1]):\n 698 |                     WHi = np.dot(W, H[:, i])\n 699 |                     if beta_loss - 1 < 0:\n 700 |                         WHi[WHi == 0] = EPSILON\n 701 |                     WHi **= beta_loss - 1\n 702 |                     WtWH[:, i] = np.dot(W.T, WHi)\n 703 |             else:\n 704 |                 WH **= beta_loss - 1\n 705 |                 WtWH = np.dot(W.T, WH)\n 706 |             denominator = WtWH\n 707 | \n 708 |     # Add L1 and L2 regularization\n 709 |     if l1_reg_H > 0:\n 710 |         denominator += l1_reg_H\n 711 |     if l2_reg_H > 0:\n 712 |         denominator = denominator + l2_reg_H * H\n 713 |     denominator[denominator == 0] = EPSILON\n 714 | \n 715 |     numerator /= denominator\n 716 |     delta_H = numerator\n 717 | \n 718 |     # gamma is in ]0, 1]\n 719 |     if gamma != 1:\n 720 |         delta_H **= gamma\n 721 | \n 722 |     return delta_H\n 723 | \n 724 | \n 725 | def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n 726 |     X,\n 727 |     W,\n 728 |     H,\n 729 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n 730 |     max_iter=200,\n 731 |     tol=1e-4,\n 732 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n 733 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n 734 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n 735 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n 736 |     update_H=True,\n 737 |     verbose=0,\n 738 | ):\n 739 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization with Multiplicative Update.\n 740 | \n 741 |     The objective function is _beta_divergence(X, WH) and is minimized with an\n 742 |     alternating minimization of W and H. Each minimization is done with a\n 743 |     Multiplicative Update.\n 744 | \n 745 |     Parameters\n 746 |     ----------\n 747 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 748 |         Constant input matrix.\n 749 | \n 750 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 751 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n 752 | \n 753 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n 754 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n 755 | \n 756 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n 757 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n 758 |         String must be in {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}.\n 759 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n 760 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n 761 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n 762 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n 763 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros.\n 764 | \n 765 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n 766 |         Number of iterations.\n 767 | \n 768 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n 769 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n 770 | \n 771 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n 772 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n 773 | \n 774 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n 775 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n 776 | \n 777 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n 778 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n 779 | \n 780 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n 781 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n 782 | \n 783 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 784 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 785 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 786 | \n 787 |     verbose : int, default=0\n 788 |         The verbosity level.\n 789 | \n 790 |     Returns\n 791 |     -------\n 792 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 793 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 794 | \n 795 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n 796 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 797 | \n 798 |     n_iter : int\n 799 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n 800 | \n 801 |     References\n 802 |     ----------\n 803 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n 804 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n 805 |     \"\"\"\n 806 |     start_time = time.time()\n 807 | \n 808 |     beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(beta_loss)\n 809 | \n 810 |     # gamma for Maximization-Minimization (MM) algorithm [Fevotte 2011]\n 811 |     if beta_loss < 1:\n 812 |         gamma = 1.0 / (2.0 - beta_loss)\n 813 |     elif beta_loss > 2:\n 814 |         gamma = 1.0 / (beta_loss - 1.0)\n 815 |     else:\n 816 |         gamma = 1.0\n 817 | \n 818 |     # used for the convergence criterion\n 819 |     error_at_init = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n 820 |     previous_error = error_at_init\n 821 | \n 822 |     H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n 823 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n 824 |         # update W\n 825 |         # H_sum, HHt and XHt are saved and reused if not update_H\n 826 |         delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n 827 |             X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, gamma, H_sum, HHt, XHt, update_H\n 828 |         )\n 829 |         W *= delta_W\n 830 | \n 831 |         # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n 832 |         if beta_loss < 1:\n 833 |             W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n 834 | \n 835 |         # update H\n 836 |         if update_H:\n 837 |             delta_H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n 838 |                 X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma\n 839 |             )\n 840 |             H *= delta_H\n 841 | \n 842 |             # These values will be recomputed since H changed\n 843 |             H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n 844 | \n 845 |             # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n 846 |             if beta_loss <= 1:\n 847 |                 H[H < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n 848 | \n 849 |         # test convergence criterion every 10 iterations\n 850 |         if tol > 0 and n_iter % 10 == 0:\n 851 |             error = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n 852 | \n 853 |             if verbose:\n 854 |                 iter_time = time.time()\n 855 |                 print(\n 856 |                     \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds, error: %f\"\n 857 |                     % (n_iter, iter_time - start_time, error)\n 858 |                 )\n 859 | \n 860 |             if (previous_error - error) / error_at_init < tol:\n 861 |                 break\n 862 |             previous_error = error\n 863 | \n 864 |     # do not print if we have already printed in the convergence test\n 865 |     if verbose and (tol == 0 or n_iter % 10 != 0):\n 866 |         end_time = time.time()\n 867 |         print(\n 868 |             \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds.\" % (n_iter, end_time - start_time)\n 869 |         )\n 870 | \n 871 |     return W, H, n_iter\n 872 | \n 873 | \n 874 | def non_negative_factorization(\n 875 |     X,\n 876 |     W=None,\n 877 |     H=None,\n 878 |     n_components=None,\n 879 |     *,\n 880 |     init=None,\n 881 |     update_H=True,\n 882 |     solver=\"cd\",\n 883 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n 884 |     tol=1e-4,\n 885 |     max_iter=200,\n 886 |     alpha=\"deprecated\",\n 887 |     alpha_W=0.0,\n 888 |     alpha_H=\"same\",\n 889 |     l1_ratio=0.0,\n 890 |     regularization=\"deprecated\",\n 891 |     random_state=None,\n 892 |     verbose=0,\n 893 |     shuffle=False,\n 894 | ):\n 895 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n 896 | \n 897 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n 898 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n 899 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n 900 | \n 901 |     The objective function is:\n 902 | \n 903 |         .. math::\n 904 | \n 905 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n 906 | \n 907 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n 908 | \n 909 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n 910 | \n 911 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n 912 | \n 913 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n 914 | \n 915 |     Where:\n 916 | \n 917 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n 918 | \n 919 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n 920 | \n 921 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n 922 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n 923 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n 924 | \n 925 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n 926 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n 927 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n 928 | \n 929 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 930 |     and H. If H is given and update_H=False, it solves for W only.\n 931 | \n 932 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n 933 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n 934 |     matrix X is transposed.\n 935 | \n 936 |     Parameters\n 937 |     ----------\n 938 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 939 |         Constant matrix.\n 940 | \n 941 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n 942 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 943 | \n 944 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 945 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 946 |         If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n 947 | \n 948 |     n_components : int, default=None\n 949 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n 950 |         are kept.\n 951 | \n 952 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n 953 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 954 | \n 955 |         Valid options:\n 956 | \n 957 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components < n_features, otherwise 'random'.\n 958 | \n 959 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 960 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 961 | \n 962 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 963 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 964 | \n 965 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 966 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 967 | \n 968 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 969 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 970 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 971 | \n 972 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H if `update_H=True`. If\n 973 |           `update_H=False`, then only custom matrix H is used.\n 974 | \n 975 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 976 |             The default value of `init` changed from 'random' to None in 0.23.\n 977 | \n 978 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 979 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 980 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 981 | \n 982 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 983 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 984 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 985 | \n 986 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n 987 |         Numerical solver to use:\n 988 | \n 989 |         - 'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver that uses Fast Hierarchical\n 990 |             Alternating Least Squares (Fast HALS).\n 991 | \n 992 |         - 'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n 993 | \n 994 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 995 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n 998 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n 999 | \n1000 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1001 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1002 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1003 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1004 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1005 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1006 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1007 | \n1008 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1009 | \n1010 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1011 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1012 | \n1013 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1014 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1015 | \n1016 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1017 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to have no\n1018 |         regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`, the\n1019 |         regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1020 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1021 | \n1022 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1023 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1024 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1025 | \n1026 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1027 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1028 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1029 | \n1030 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1031 | \n1032 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1033 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1034 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1035 |         `alpha_W`.\n1036 | \n1037 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1038 | \n1039 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1040 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1041 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1042 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1043 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1044 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1045 | \n1046 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation'}, default=None\n1047 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n1118 | \n1119 | \n1120 | class NMF(_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n1121 |     \"\"\"Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n1122 | \n1123 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n1124 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n1125 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n1126 | \n1127 |     The objective function is:\n1128 | \n1129 |         .. math::\n1130 | \n1131 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n1132 | \n1133 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n1134 | \n1135 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n1136 | \n1137 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n1138 | \n1139 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n1140 | \n1141 |     Where:\n1142 | \n1143 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n1144 | \n1145 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n1146 | \n1147 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}` may represent\n1148 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n1149 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n1150 | \n1151 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n1152 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n1153 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n1154 | \n1155 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n1156 |     and H.\n1157 | \n1158 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n1159 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n1160 |     matrix X is transposed.\n1161 | \n1162 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <NMF>`.\n1163 | \n1164 |     Parameters\n1165 |     ----------\n1166 |     n_components : int, default=None\n1167 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n1168 |         are kept.\n1169 | \n1170 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n1171 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n1172 |         Default: None.\n1173 |         Valid options:\n1174 | \n1175 |         - `None`: 'nndsvda' if n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features),\n1176 |           otherwise random.\n1177 | \n1178 |         - `'random'`: non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n1179 |           sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n1180 | \n1181 |         - `'nndsvd'`: Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n1182 |           initialization (better for sparseness)\n1183 | \n1184 |         - `'nndsvda'`: NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n1185 |           (better when sparsity is not desired)\n1186 | \n1187 |         - `'nndsvdar'` NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n1188 |           (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n1189 |           for when sparsity is not desired)\n1190 | \n1191 |         - `'custom'`: use custom matrices W and H\n1192 | \n1193 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n1194 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n1195 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n1196 | \n1197 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n1198 |         Numerical solver to use:\n1199 |         'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver.\n1200 |         'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n1201 | \n1202 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1203 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n1204 | \n1205 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1206 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n1207 | \n1208 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1209 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1210 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1211 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1212 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1213 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1214 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1215 | \n1216 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1217 | \n1218 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1219 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1220 | \n1221 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1222 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1223 | \n1224 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1225 |         Used for initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1226 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1227 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1228 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1229 | \n1230 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1231 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to\n1232 |         have no regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`,\n1233 |         the regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1234 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1235 | \n1236 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1237 |            *alpha* used in the Coordinate Descent solver.\n1238 | \n1239 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1240 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1241 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1242 | \n1243 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1244 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1245 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1246 | \n1247 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1248 | \n1249 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1250 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1251 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1252 |         `alpha_W`.\n1253 | \n1254 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1255 | \n1256 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1257 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1258 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1259 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1260 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1261 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1262 | \n1263 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1264 |            Regularization parameter *l1_ratio* used in the Coordinate Descent\n1265 |            solver.\n1266 | \n1267 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1268 |         Whether to be verbose.\n1269 | \n1270 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1271 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1272 | \n1273 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1274 |            *shuffle* parameter used in the Coordinate Descent solver.\n1275 | \n1276 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation', None}, \\\n1277 |                      default='both'\n1278 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1279 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1280 | \n1281 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1282 | \n1283 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1284 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1285 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1286 | \n1287 |     Attributes\n1288 |     ----------\n1289 |     components_ : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1290 |         Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n1291 | \n1292 |     n_components_ : int\n1293 |         The number of components. It is same as the `n_components` parameter\n1294 |         if it was given. Otherwise, it will be same as the number of\n1295 |         features.\n1296 | \n1297 |     reconstruction_err_ : float\n1298 |         Frobenius norm of the matrix difference, or beta-divergence, between\n1299 |         the training data ``X`` and the reconstructed data ``WH`` from\n1300 |         the fitted model.\n1301 | \n1302 |     n_iter_ : int\n1303 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1304 | \n1305 |     n_features_in_ : int\n1306 |         Number of features seen during :term:`fit`.\n1307 | \n1308 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1309 | \n1310 |     feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n1311 |         Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Defined only when `X`\n1312 |         has feature names that are all strings.\n1313 | \n1314 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1315 | \n1316 |     See Also\n1317 |     --------\n1318 |     DictionaryLearning : Find a dictionary that sparsely encodes data.\n1319 |     MiniBatchSparsePCA : Mini-batch Sparse Principal Components Analysis.\n1320 |     PCA : Principal component analysis.\n1321 |     SparseCoder : Find a sparse representation of data from a fixed,\n1322 |         precomputed dictionary.\n1323 |     SparsePCA : Sparse Principal Components Analysis.\n1324 |     TruncatedSVD : Dimensionality reduction using truncated SVD.\n1325 | \n1326 |     References\n1327 |     ----------\n1328 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1329 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1330 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1331 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1332 | \n1333 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1334 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1335 | \n1336 |     Examples\n1337 |     --------\n1338 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1339 |     >>> X = np.array([[1, 1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1340 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import NMF\n1341 |     >>> model = NMF(n_components=2, init='random', random_state=0)\n1342 |     >>> W = model.fit_transform(X)\n1343 |     >>> H = model.components_\n1344 |     \"\"\"\n1345 | \n1346 |     def __init__(\n1347 |         self,\n1348 |         n_components=None,\n1349 |         *,\n1350 |         init=None,\n1351 |         solver=\"cd\",\n1352 |         beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n1353 |         tol=1e-4,\n1354 |         max_iter=200,\n1355 |         random_state=None,\n1356 |         alpha=\"deprecated\",\n1357 |         alpha_W=0.0,\n1358 |         alpha_H=\"same\",\n1359 |         l1_ratio=0.0,\n1360 |         verbose=0,\n1361 |         shuffle=False,\n1362 |         regularization=\"deprecated\",\n1363 |     ):\n1364 |         self.n_components = n_components\n1365 |         self.init = init\n1366 |         self.solver = solver\n1367 |         self.beta_loss = beta_loss\n1368 |         self.tol = tol\n1369 |         self.max_iter = max_iter\n1370 |         self.random_state = random_state\n1371 |         self.alpha = alpha\n1372 |         self.alpha_W = alpha_W\n1373 |         self.alpha_H = alpha_H\n1374 |         self.l1_ratio = l1_ratio\n1375 |         self.verbose = verbose\n1376 |         self.shuffle = shuffle\n1377 |         self.regularization = regularization\n1378 | \n1379 |     def _more_tags(self):\n1380 |         return {\"requires_positive_X\": True}\n1381 | \n1382 |     def _check_params(self, X):\n1383 |         # n_components\n1384 |         self._n_components = self.n_components\n1385 |         if self._n_components is None:\n1386 |             self._n_components = X.shape[1]\n1387 |         if (\n1388 |             not isinstance(self._n_components, numbers.Integral)\n1389 |             or self._n_components <= 0\n1390 |         ):\n1391 |             raise ValueError(\n1392 |                 \"Number of components must be a positive integer; got \"\n1393 |                 f\"(n_components={self._n_components!r})\"\n1394 |             )\n1395 | \n1396 |         # max_iter\n1397 |         if not isinstance(self.max_iter, numbers.Integral) or self.max_iter < 0:\n1398 |             raise ValueError(\n1399 |                 \"Maximum number of iterations must be a positive \"\n1400 |                 f\"integer; got (max_iter={self.max_iter!r})\"\n1401 |             )\n1402 | \n1403 |         # tol\n1404 |         if not isinstance(self.tol, numbers.Number) or self.tol < 0:\n1405 |             raise ValueError(\n1406 |                 \"Tolerance for stopping criteria must be positive; got \"\n1407 |                 f\"(tol={self.tol!r})\"\n1408 |             )\n1409 | \n1410 |         # beta_loss\n1411 |         self._beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(self.beta_loss)\n1412 | \n1413 |         # solver\n1414 |         allowed_solver = (\"cd\", \"mu\")\n1415 |         if self.solver not in allowed_solver:\n1416 |             raise ValueError(\n1417 |                 f\"Invalid solver parameter: got {self.solver!r} instead of one of \"\n1418 |                 f\"{allowed_solver}\"\n1419 |             )\n1420 |         if self.solver != \"mu\" and self.beta_loss not in (2, \"frobenius\"):\n1421 |             # 'mu' is the only solver that handles other beta losses than 'frobenius'\n1422 |             raise ValueError(\n1423 |                 f\"Invalid beta_loss parameter: solver {self.solver!r} does not handle \"\n1424 |                 f\"beta_loss = {self.beta_loss!r}\"\n1425 |             )\n1426 |         if self.solver == \"mu\" and self.init == \"nndsvd\":\n1427 |             warnings.warn(\n1428 |                 \"The multiplicative update ('mu') solver cannot update \"\n1429 |                 \"zeros present in the initialization, and so leads to \"\n1430 |                 \"poorer results when used jointly with init='nndsvd'. \"\n1431 |                 \"You may try init='nndsvda' or init='nndsvdar' instead.\",\n1432 |                 UserWarning,\n1433 |             )\n1434 | \n1435 |         # alpha and regularization are deprecated in favor of alpha_W and alpha_H\n1436 |         # TODO clean up in 1.2\n1437 |         if self.alpha != \"deprecated\":\n1438 |             warnings.warn(\n1439 |                 \"`alpha` was deprecated in version 1.0 and will be removed \"\n1440 |                 \"in 1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead\",\n1441 |                 FutureWarning,\n1442 |             )\n1443 |             alpha = self.alpha\n1444 |         else:\n1445 |             alpha = 0.0\n1446 | \n1447 |         if self.regularization != \"deprecated\":\n1448 |             warnings.warn(\n1449 |                 \"`regularization` was deprecated in version 1.0 and will be \"\n1450 |                 \"removed in 1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead\",\n1451 |                 FutureWarning,\n1452 |             )\n1453 |             allowed_regularization = (\"both\", \"components\", \"transformation\", None)\n1454 |             if self.regularization not in allowed_regularization:\n1455 |                 raise ValueError(\n1456 |                     f\"Invalid regularization parameter: got {self.regularization!r} \"\n1457 |                     f\"instead of one of {allowed_regularization}\"\n1458 |                 )\n1459 |             regularization = self.regularization\n1460 |         else:\n1461 |             regularization = \"both\"\n1462 | \n1463 |         (\n1464 |             self._l1_reg_W,\n1465 |             self._l1_reg_H,\n1466 |             self._l2_reg_W,\n1467 |             self._l2_reg_H,\n1468 |         ) = _compute_regularization(\n1469 |             alpha, self.alpha_W, self.alpha_H, self.l1_ratio, regularization\n1470 |         )\n1471 | \n1472 |         return self\n1473 | \n1474 |     def _check_w_h(self, X, W, H, update_H):\n1475 |         # check W and H, or initialize them\n1476 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n1477 |         if self.init == \"custom\" and update_H:\n1478 |             _check_init(H, (self._n_components, n_features), \"NMF (input H)\")\n1479 |             _check_init(W, (n_samples, self._n_components), \"NMF (input W)\")\n1480 |             if H.dtype != X.dtype or W.dtype != X.dtype:\n1481 |                 raise TypeError(\n1482 |                     \"H and W should have the same dtype as X. Got \"\n1483 |                     \"H.dtype = {} and W.dtype = {}.\".format(H.dtype, W.dtype)\n1484 |                 )\n1485 |         elif not update_H:\n1486 |             _check_init(H, (self._n_components, n_features), \"NMF (input H)\")\n1487 |             if H.dtype != X.dtype:\n1488 |                 raise TypeError(\n1489 |                     \"H should have the same dtype as X. Got H.dtype = {}.\".format(\n1490 |                         H.dtype\n1491 |                     )\n1492 |                 )\n1493 |             # 'mu' solver should not be initialized by zeros\n1494 |             if self.solver == \"mu\":\n1495 |                 avg = np.sqrt(X.mean() / self._n_components)\n1496 |                 W = np.full((n_samples, self._n_components), avg, dtype=X.dtype)\n1497 |             else:\n1498 |                 W = np.zeros((n_samples, self._n_components), dtype=X.dtype)\n1499 |         else:\n1500 |             W, H = _initialize_nmf(\n1501 |                 X, self._n_components, init=self.init, random_state=self.random_state\n1502 |             )\n1503 |         return W, H\n1504 | \n1505 |     def _scale_regularization(self, X):\n1506 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n1507 |         if self.alpha_W != 0 or self.alpha_H != \"same\":\n1508 |             # if alpha_W or alpha_H is not left to its default value we ignore alpha\n1509 |             # and regularization, and we scale the regularization terms.\n1510 |             l1_reg_W = n_features * self._l1_reg_W\n1511 |             l1_reg_H = n_samples * self._l1_reg_H\n1512 |             l2_reg_W = n_features * self._l2_reg_W\n1513 |             l2_reg_H = n_samples * self._l2_reg_H\n1514 |         else:\n1515 |             # Otherwise we keep the old behavior with no scaling\n1516 |             # TODO remove in 1.2\n1517 |             l1_reg_W = self._l1_reg_W\n1518 |             l1_reg_H = self._l1_reg_H\n1519 |             l2_reg_W = self._l2_reg_W\n1520 |             l2_reg_H = self._l2_reg_H\n1521 | \n1522 |         return l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H\n1523 | \n1524 |     def fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None):\n1525 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n1526 | \n1527 |         This is more efficient than calling fit followed by transform.\n1528 | \n1529 |         Parameters\n1530 |         ----------\n1531 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1532 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1533 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1534 | \n1535 |         y : Ignored\n1536 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n1537 | \n1538 |         W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1539 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1540 | \n1541 |         H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1542 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1543 | \n1544 |         Returns\n1545 |         -------\n1546 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1547 |             Transformed data.\n1548 |         \"\"\"\n1549 |         X = self._validate_data(\n1550 |             X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32]\n1551 |         )\n1552 | \n1553 |         with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1554 |             W, H, n_iter = self._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H)\n1555 | \n1556 |         self.reconstruction_err_ = _beta_divergence(\n1557 |             X, W, H, self._beta_loss, square_root=True\n1558 |         )\n1559 | \n1560 |         self.n_components_ = H.shape[0]\n1561 |         self.components_ = H\n1562 |         self.n_iter_ = n_iter\n1563 | \n1564 |         return W\n1565 | \n1566 |     def _fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None, update_H=True):\n1567 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n1568 | \n1569 |         Parameters\n1570 |         ----------\n1571 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1572 |             Data matrix to be decomposed\n1573 | \n1574 |         y : Ignored\n1575 | \n1576 |         W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1577 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1578 | \n1579 |         H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1580 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1581 |             If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n1582 | \n1583 |         update_H : bool, default=True\n1584 |             If True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses,\n1585 |             this corresponds to a call to the 'fit_transform' method.\n1586 |             If False, only W will be estimated, this corresponds to a call\n1587 |             to the 'transform' method.\n1588 | \n1589 |         Returns\n1590 |         -------\n1591 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1592 |             Transformed data.\n1593 | \n1594 |         H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1595 |             Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n1596 | \n1597 |         n_iter_ : int\n1598 |             Actual number of iterations.\n1599 |         \"\"\"\n1600 |         check_non_negative(X, \"NMF (input X)\")\n1601 | \n1602 |         # check parameters\n1603 |         self._check_params(X)\n1604 | \n1605 |         if X.min() == 0 and self._beta_loss <= 0:\n1606 |             raise ValueError(\n1607 |                 \"When beta_loss <= 0 and X contains zeros, \"\n1608 |                 \"the solver may diverge. Please add small values \"\n1609 |                 \"to X, or use a positive beta_loss.\"\n1610 |             )\n1611 | \n1612 |         # initialize or check W and H\n1613 |         W, H = self._check_w_h(X, W, H, update_H)\n1614 | \n1615 |         # scale the regularization terms\n1616 |         l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H = self._scale_regularization(X)\n1617 | \n1618 |         if self.solver == \"cd\":\n1619 |             W, H, n_iter = _fit_coordinate_descent(\n1620 |                 X,\n1621 |                 W,\n1622 |                 H,\n1623 |                 self.tol,\n1624 |                 self.max_iter,\n1625 |                 l1_reg_W,\n1626 |                 l1_reg_H,\n1627 |                 l2_reg_W,\n1628 |                 l2_reg_H,\n1629 |                 update_H=update_H,\n1630 |                 verbose=self.verbose,\n1631 |                 shuffle=self.shuffle,\n1632 |                 random_state=self.random_state,\n1633 |             )\n1634 |         elif self.solver == \"mu\":\n1635 |             W, H, n_iter = _fit_multiplicative_update(\n1636 |                 X,\n1637 |                 W,\n1638 |                 H,\n1639 |                 self._beta_loss,\n1640 |                 self.max_iter,\n1641 |                 self.tol,\n1642 |                 l1_reg_W,\n1643 |                 l1_reg_H,\n1644 |                 l2_reg_W,\n1645 |                 l2_reg_H,\n1646 |                 update_H=update_H,\n1647 |                 verbose=self.verbose,\n1648 |             )\n1649 |         else:\n1650 |             raise ValueError(\"Invalid solver parameter '%s'.\" % self.solver)\n1651 | \n1652 |         if n_iter == self.max_iter and self.tol > 0:\n1653 |             warnings.warn(\n1654 |                 \"Maximum number of iterations %d reached. Increase \"\n1655 |                 \"it to improve convergence.\"\n1656 |                 % self.max_iter,\n1657 |                 ConvergenceWarning,\n1658 |             )\n1659 | \n1660 |         return W, H, n_iter\n1661 | \n1662 |     def fit(self, X, y=None, **params):\n1663 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X.\n1664 | \n1665 |         Parameters\n1666 |         ----------\n1667 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1668 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1669 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1670 | \n1671 |         y : Ignored\n1672 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n1673 | \n1674 |         **params : kwargs\n1675 |             Parameters (keyword arguments) and values passed to\n1676 |             the fit_transform instance.\n1677 | \n1678 |         Returns\n1679 |         -------\n1680 |         self : object\n1681 |             Returns the instance itself.\n1682 |         \"\"\"\n1683 |         self.fit_transform(X, **params)\n1684 |         return self\n1685 | \n1686 |     def transform(self, X):\n1687 |         \"\"\"Transform the data X according to the fitted NMF model.\n1688 | \n1689 |         Parameters\n1690 |         ----------\n1691 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1692 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1693 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1694 | \n1695 |         Returns\n1696 |         -------\n1697 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1698 |             Transformed data.\n1699 |         \"\"\"\n1700 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n1701 |         X = self._validate_data(\n1702 |             X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32], reset=False\n1703 |         )\n1704 | \n1705 |         with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1706 |             W, *_ = self._fit_transform(X, H=self.components_, update_H=False)\n1707 | \n1708 |         return W\n1709 | \n1710 |     def inverse_transform(self, W):\n1711 |         \"\"\"Transform data back to its original space.\n1712 | \n1713 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n1714 | \n1715 |         Parameters\n1716 |         ----------\n1717 |         W : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1718 |             Transformed data matrix.\n1719 | \n1720 |         Returns\n1721 |         -------\n1722 |         X : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1723 |             Returns a data matrix of the original shape.\n1724 |         \"\"\"\n1725 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n1726 |         return np.dot(W, self.components_)\n1727 | \n1728 |     @property\n1729 |     def _n_features_out(self):\n1730 |         \"\"\"Number of transformed output features.\"\"\"\n1731 |         return self.components_.shape[0]\n1732 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of `_nmf.py`, the following locations are identified as potentially relevant for implementing an online version of NMF that supports different beta divergences:\n\n```\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1292\nclass: NMF\nfunction: fit\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1652\nclass: NMF\nfunction: _fit_transform\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 805\nfunction: _beta_divergence\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1411\nclass: NMF\nfunction: _check_params\n\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py\nline: 1701\nclass: NMF\nfunction: transform\n```\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 60064, "completion_tokens": 350}, "time": 65.34930300712585}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-14800", "found_files": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py", "sklearn/datasets/_base.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response", "sklearn/ensemble/_gb_losses.py:MultinomialDeviance", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml", "sklearn/cluster/_kmeans.py:KMeans", "sklearn/datasets/_base.py:_fetch_remote"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response", "sklearn/ensemble/_gb_losses.py:MultinomialDeviance.init_estimator", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml", "sklearn/cluster/_kmeans.py:KMeans._transform", "sklearn/datasets/_base.py:_fetch_remote"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the search results, the `_fetch_remote` function in `sklearn/datasets/_base.py` uses SHA256 checksums for integrity checks. Since the issue request is for MD5 checksum validation, we should implement similar functionality in the `_download_data_to_bunch` function in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` using `hashlib.md5`.\n\nHere are the final locations requiring modification:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491\nfunction: _load_arff_response\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 690\nfunction: fetch_openml\n\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py\nline: 1157\nfunction: _fetch_remote\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "acb8ac5145cfd88fdbd2d381b34883b2c212c8c5", "problem_statement": "Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in fetch_openml\n`fetch_openml` downloads the `data_set_description` from openml.org which includes an `md5_checksum` field. This should correspond to the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data. We should (optionally?) use `hashlib` to verify that the MD5 matches.\n", "patch": "diff --git a/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst b/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\nindex a647de6dac628..ba137fb76698f 100644\n--- a/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\n+++ b/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\n@@ -224,6 +224,11 @@ Changelog\n   :meth:`tree.DecisionTreeRegressor.fit`, and has not effect.\n   :pr:`17614` by :user:`Juan Carlos Alfaro Jim\u00e9nez <alfaro96>`.\n \n+:mod:`sklearn.datasets`\n+.......................\n+- |Feature| :func:`datasets.fetch_openml` now validates md5checksum of arff\n+  files downloaded or cached to ensure data integrity.\n+  :pr:`14800` by :user:`Shashank Singh <shashanksingh28>` and `Joel Nothman`_.\n \n Code and Documentation Contributors\n -----------------------------------\ndiff --git a/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py b/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nindex 6b537c09c59ad..d2ce5d471f8f7 100644\n--- a/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\n+++ b/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@\n import json\n import os\n import shutil\n+import hashlib\n from os.path import join\n from warnings import warn\n from contextlib import closing\n@@ -492,7 +493,8 @@ def _load_arff_response(\n     url: str,\n     data_home: Optional[str],\n     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n-    parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n+    parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple],\n+    md5_checksum: str\n ) -> Tuple:\n     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n@@ -500,10 +502,32 @@ def _load_arff_response(\n     with closing(response):\n         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n         # generator is iterated.\n-        arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n+        actual_md5_checksum = hashlib.md5()\n+\n+        def _stream_checksum_generator(response):\n+            for line in response:\n+                actual_md5_checksum.update(line)\n+                yield line.decode('utf-8')\n+\n+        stream = _stream_checksum_generator(response)\n+\n+        arff = _arff.load(stream,\n                           return_type=return_type,\n                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n-        return parse_arff(arff)\n+\n+        parsed_arff = parse_arff(arff)\n+\n+        # consume remaining stream, if early exited\n+        for _ in stream:\n+            pass\n+\n+        if actual_md5_checksum.hexdigest() != md5_checksum:\n+            raise ValueError(\"md5 checksum of local file for \" + url +\n+                             \" does not match description. \"\n+                             \"Downloaded file could have been modified / \"\n+                             \"corrupted, clean cache and retry...\")\n+\n+        return parsed_arff\n \n \n def _download_data_to_bunch(\n@@ -515,7 +539,8 @@ def _download_data_to_bunch(\n     features_list: List,\n     data_columns: List[int],\n     target_columns: List,\n-    shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n+    shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]],\n+    md5_checksum: str\n ):\n     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n     \"\"\"\n@@ -609,7 +634,8 @@ def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n                              return_type=return_type,\n                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n-                             parse_arff=parse_arff)\n+                             parse_arff=parse_arff,\n+                             md5_checksum=md5_checksum)\n     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n \n     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n@@ -883,7 +909,9 @@ def fetch_openml(\n                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n-                                    data_columns=data_columns)\n+                                    data_columns=data_columns,\n+                                    md5_checksum=data_description[\n+                                        \"md5_checksum\"])\n \n     if return_X_y:\n         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 170521, "completion_tokens": 705}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may