ter=entity_names>['sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py', 'sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py`.\n```\n   1 | import numbers\n   2 | from itertools import chain\n   3 | from math import ceil\n   4 | \n   5 | import numpy as np\n   6 | from scipy import sparse\n   7 | from scipy.stats.mstats import mquantiles\n   8 | from joblib import Parallel\n   9 | \n  10 | from .. import partial_dependence\n  11 | from ...base import is_regressor\n  12 | from ...utils import check_array\n  13 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support  # noqa\n  14 | from ...utils import check_random_state\n  15 | from ...utils import _safe_indexing\n  16 | from ...utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n  17 | from ...utils.fixes import delayed\n  18 | \n  19 | \n  20 | def plot_partial_dependence(\n  21 |     estimator,\n  22 |     X,\n  23 |     features,\n  24 |     *,\n  25 |     feature_names=None,\n  26 |     target=None,\n  27 |     response_method=\"auto\",\n  28 |     n_cols=3,\n  29 |     grid_resolution=100,\n  30 |     percentiles=(0.05, 0.95),\n  31 |     method=\"auto\",\n  32 |     n_jobs=None,\n  33 |     verbose=0,\n  34 |     line_kw=None,\n  35 |     ice_lines_kw=None,\n  36 |     pd_line_kw=None,\n  37 |     contour_kw=None,\n  38 |     ax=None,\n  39 |     kind=\"average\",\n  40 |     subsample=1000,\n  41 |     random_state=None,\n  42 | ):\n  43 |     \"\"\"Partial dependence (PD) and individual conditional expectation (ICE)\n  44 |     plots.\n  45 | \n  46 |     Partial dependence plots, individual conditional expectation plots or an\n  47 |     overlay of both of them can be plotted by setting the ``kind``\n  48 |     parameter.\n  49 |     The ``len(features)`` plots are arranged in a grid with ``n_cols``\n  50 |     columns. Two-way partial dependence plots are plotted as contour plots. The\n  51 |     deciles of the feature values will be shown with tick marks on the x-axes\n  52 |     for one-way plots, and on both axes for two-way plots.\n  53 | \n  54 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <partial_dependence>`.\n  55 | \n  56 |     .. note::\n  57 | \n  58 |         :func:`plot_partial_dependence` does not support using the same axes\n  59 |         with multiple calls. To plot the the partial dependence for multiple\n  60 |         estimators, please pass the axes created by the first call to the\n  61 |         second call::\n  62 | \n  63 |           >>> from sklearn.inspection import plot_partial_dependence\n  64 |           >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_friedman1\n  65 |           >>> from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n  66 |           >>> from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n  67 |           >>> X, y = make_friedman1()\n  68 |           >>> est1 = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\n  69 |           >>> est2 = RandomForestRegressor().fit(X, y)\n  70 |           >>> disp1 = plot_partial_dependence(est1, X,\n  71 |           ...                                 [1, 2])\n  72 |           >>> disp2 = plot_partial_dependence(est2, X, [1, 2],\n  73 |           ...                                 ax=disp1.axes_)\n  74 | \n  75 |     .. warning::\n  76 | \n  77 |         For :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingClassifier` and\n  78 |         :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`, the\n  79 |         `'recursion'` method (used by default) will not account for the `init`\n  80 |         predictor of the boosting process. In practice, this will produce\n  81 |         the same values as `'brute'` up to a constant offset in the target\n  82 |         response, provided that `init` is a constant estimator (which is the\n  83 |         default). However, if `init` is not a constant estimator, the\n  84 |         partial dependence values are incorrect for `'recursion'` because the\n  85 |         offset will be sample-dependent. It is preferable to use the `'brute'`\n  86 |         method. Note that this only applies to\n  87 |         :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingClassifier` and\n  88 |         :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`, not to\n  89 |         :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingClassifier` and\n  90 |         :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor`.\n  91 | \n  92 |     Parameters\n  93 |     ----------\n  94 |     estimator : BaseEstimator\n  95 |         A fitted estimator object implementing :term:`predict`,\n  96 |         :term:`predict_proba`, or :term:`decision_function`.\n  97 |         Multioutput-multiclass classifiers are not supported.\n  98 | \n  99 |     X : {array-like or dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 100 |         ``X`` is used to generate a grid of values for the target\n 101 |         ``features`` (where the partial dependence will be evaluated), and\n 102 |         also to generate values for the complement features when the\n 103 |         `method` is `'brute'`.\n 104 | \n 105 |     features : list of {int, str, pair of int, pair of str}\n 106 |         The target features for which to create the PDPs.\n 107 |         If `features[i]` is an integer or a string, a one-way PDP is created;\n 108 |         if `features[i]` is a tuple, a two-way PDP is created (only supported\n 109 |         with `kind='average'`). Each tuple must be of size 2.\n 110 |         if any entry is a string, then it must be in ``feature_names``.\n 111 | \n 112 |     feature_names : array-like of shape (n_features,), dtype=str, default=None\n 113 |         Name of each feature; `feature_names[i]` holds the name of the feature\n 114 |         with index `i`.\n 115 |         By default, the name of the feature corresponds to their numerical\n 116 |         index for NumPy array and their column name for pandas dataframe.\n 117 | \n 118 |     target : int, default=None\n 119 |         - In a multiclass setting, specifies the class for which the PDPs\n 120 |           should be computed. Note that for binary classification, the\n 121 |           positive class (index 1) is always used.\n 122 |         - In a multioutput setting, specifies the task for which the PDPs\n 123 |           should be computed.\n 124 | \n 125 |         Ignored in binary classification or classical regression settings.\n 126 | \n 127 |     response_method : {'auto', 'predict_proba', 'decision_function'}, \\\n 128 |             default='auto'\n 129 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n 130 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. For regressors\n 131 |         this parameter is ignored and the response is always the output of\n 132 |         :term:`predict`. By default, :term:`predict_proba` is tried first\n 133 |         and we revert to :term:`decision_function` if it doesn't exist. If\n 134 |         ``method`` is `'recursion'`, the response is always the output of\n 135 |         :term:`decision_function`.\n 136 | \n 137 |     n_cols : int, default=3\n 138 |         The maximum number of columns in the grid plot. Only active when `ax`\n 139 |         is a single axis or `None`.\n 140 | \n 141 |     grid_resolution : int, default=100\n 142 |         The number of equally spaced points on the axes of the plots, for each\n 143 |         target feature.\n 144 | \n 145 |     percentiles : tuple of float, default=(0.05, 0.95)\n 146 |         The lower and upper percentile used to create the extreme values\n 147 |         for the PDP axes. Must be in [0, 1].\n 148 | \n 149 |     method : str, default='auto'\n 150 |         The method used to calculate the averaged predictions:\n 151 | \n 152 |         - `'recursion'` is only supported for some tree-based estimators\n 153 |           (namely\n 154 |           :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingClassifier`,\n 155 |           :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`,\n 156 |           :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingClassifier`,\n 157 |           :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor`,\n 158 |           :class:`~sklearn.tree.DecisionTreeRegressor`,\n 159 |           :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor`\n 160 |           but is more efficient in terms of speed.\n 161 |           With this method, the target response of a\n 162 |           classifier is always the decision function, not the predicted\n 163 |           probabilities. Since the `'recursion'` method implicitely computes\n 164 |           the average of the ICEs by design, it is not compatible with ICE and\n 165 |           thus `kind` must be `'average'`.\n 166 | \n 167 |         - `'brute'` is supported for any estimator, but is more\n 168 |           computationally intensive.\n 169 | \n 170 |         - `'auto'`: the `'recursion'` is used for estimators that support it,\n 171 |           and `'brute'` is used otherwise.\n 172 | \n 173 |         Please see :ref:`this note <pdp_method_differences>` for\n 174 |         differences between the `'brute'` and `'recursion'` method.\n 175 | \n 176 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n 177 |         The number of CPUs to use to compute the partial dependences.\n 178 |         Computation is parallelized over features specified by the `features`\n 179 |         parameter.\n 180 | \n 181 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n 182 |         ``-1`` means using all processors. See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>`\n 183 |         for more details.\n 184 | \n 185 |     verbose : int, default=0\n 186 |         Verbose output during PD computations.\n 187 | \n 188 |     line_kw : dict, default=None\n 189 |         Dict with keywords passed to the ``matplotlib.pyplot.plot`` call.\n 190 |         For one-way partial dependence plots. It can be used to define common\n 191 |         properties for both `ice_lines_kw` and `pdp_line_kw`.\n 192 | \n 193 |     ice_lines_kw : dict, default=None\n 194 |         Dictionary with keywords passed to the `matplotlib.pyplot.plot` call.\n 195 |         For ICE lines in the one-way partial dependence plots.\n 196 |         The key value pairs defined in `ice_lines_kw` takes priority over\n 197 |         `line_kw`.\n 198 | \n 199 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 200 | \n 201 |     pd_line_kw : dict, default=None\n 202 |         Dictionary with keywords passed to the `matplotlib.pyplot.plot` call.\n 203 |         For partial dependence in one-way partial dependence plots.\n 204 |         The key value pairs defined in `pd_line_kw` takes priority over\n 205 |         `line_kw`.\n 206 | \n 207 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 208 | \n 209 |     contour_kw : dict, default=None\n 210 |         Dict with keywords passed to the ``matplotlib.pyplot.contourf`` call.\n 211 |         For two-way partial dependence plots.\n 212 | \n 213 |     ax : Matplotlib axes or array-like of Matplotlib axes, default=None\n 214 |         - If a single axis is passed in, it is treated as a bounding axes\n 215 |           and a grid of partial dependence plots will be drawn within\n 216 |           these bounds. The `n_cols` parameter controls the number of\n 217 |           columns in the grid.\n 218 |         - If an array-like of axes are passed in, the partial dependence\n 219 |           plots will be drawn directly into these axes.\n 220 |         - If `None`, a figure and a bounding axes is created and treated\n 221 |           as the single axes case.\n 222 | \n 223 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n 224 | \n 225 |     kind : {'average', 'individual', 'both'}, default='average'\n 226 |         Whether to plot the partial dependence averaged across all the samples\n 227 |         in the dataset or one line per sample or both.\n 228 | \n 229 |         - ``kind='average'`` results in the traditional PD plot;\n 230 |         - ``kind='individual'`` results in the ICE plot.\n 231 | \n 232 |        Note that the fast ``method='recursion'`` option is only available for\n 233 |        ``kind='average'``. Plotting individual dependencies requires using the\n 234 |        slower ``method='brute'`` option.\n 235 | \n 236 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 237 | \n 238 |     subsample : float, int or None, default=1000\n 239 |         Sampling for ICE curves when `kind` is 'individual' or 'both'.\n 240 |         If `float`, should be between 0.0 and 1.0 and represent the proportion\n 241 |         of the dataset to be used to plot ICE curves. If `int`, represents the\n 242 |         absolute number samples to use.\n 243 | \n 244 |         Note that the full dataset is still used to calculate averaged partial\n 245 |         dependence when `kind='both'`.\n 246 | \n 247 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 248 | \n 249 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n 250 |         Controls the randomness of the selected samples when subsamples is not\n 251 |         `None` and `kind` is either `'both'` or `'individual'`.\n 252 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>` for details.\n 253 | \n 254 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 255 | \n 256 |     Returns\n 257 |     -------\n 258 |     display : :class:`~sklearn.inspection.PartialDependenceDisplay`\n 259 | \n 260 |     See Also\n 261 |     --------\n 262 |     partial_dependence : Compute Partial Dependence values.\n 263 |     PartialDependenceDisplay : Partial Dependence visualization.\n 264 | \n 265 |     Examples\n 266 |     --------\n 267 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 268 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_friedman1\n 269 |     >>> from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingRegressor\n 270 |     >>> from sklearn.inspection import plot_partial_dependence\n 271 |     >>> X, y = make_friedman1()\n 272 |     >>> clf = GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators=10).fit(X, y)\n 273 |     >>> plot_partial_dependence(clf, X, [0, (0, 1)])\n 274 |     <...>\n 275 |     >>> plt.show()\n 276 |     \"\"\"\n 277 |     check_matplotlib_support(\"plot_partial_dependence\")  # noqa\n 278 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt  # noqa\n 279 | \n 280 |     # set target_idx for multi-class estimators\n 281 |     if hasattr(estimator, \"classes_\") and np.size(estimator.classes_) > 2:\n 282 |         if target is None:\n 283 |             raise ValueError(\"target must be specified for multi-class\")\n 284 |         target_idx = np.searchsorted(estimator.classes_, target)\n 285 |         if (\n 286 |             not (0 <= target_idx < len(estimator.classes_))\n 287 |             or estimator.classes_[target_idx] != target\n 288 |         ):\n 289 |             raise ValueError(\"target not in est.classes_, got {}\".format(target))\n 290 |     else:\n 291 |         # regression and binary classification\n 292 |         target_idx = 0\n 293 | \n 294 |     # Use check_array only on lists and other non-array-likes / sparse. Do not\n 295 |     # convert DataFrame into a NumPy array.\n 296 |     if not (hasattr(X, \"__array__\") or sparse.issparse(X)):\n 297 |         X = check_array(X, force_all_finite=\"allow-nan\", dtype=object)\n 298 |     n_features = X.shape[1]\n 299 | \n 300 |     # convert feature_names to list\n 301 |     if feature_names is None:\n 302 |         if hasattr(X, \"loc\"):\n 303 |             # get the column names for a pandas dataframe\n 304 |             feature_names = X.columns.tolist()\n 305 |         else:\n 306 |             # define a list of numbered indices for a numpy array\n 307 |             feature_names = [str(i) for i in range(n_features)]\n 308 |     elif hasattr(feature_names, \"tolist\"):\n 309 |         # convert numpy array or pandas index to a list\n 310 |         feature_names = feature_names.tolist()\n 311 |     if len(set(feature_names)) != len(feature_names):\n 312 |         raise ValueError(\"feature_names should not contain duplicates.\")\n 313 | \n 314 |     def convert_feature(fx):\n 315 |         if isinstance(fx, str):\n 316 |             try:\n 317 |                 fx = feature_names.index(fx)\n 318 |             except ValueError as e:\n 319 |                 raise ValueError(\"Feature %s not in feature_names\" % fx) from e\n 320 |         return int(fx)\n 321 | \n 322 |     # convert features into a seq of int tuples\n 323 |     tmp_features = []\n 324 |     for fxs in features:\n 325 |         if isinstance(fxs, (numbers.Integral, str)):\n 326 |             fxs = (fxs,)\n 327 |         try:\n 328 |             fxs = tuple(convert_feature(fx) for fx in fxs)\n 329 |         except TypeError as e:\n 330 |             raise ValueError(\n 331 |                 \"Each entry in features must be either an int, \"\n 332 |                 \"a string, or an iterable of size at most 2.\"\n 333 |             ) from e\n 334 |         if not 1 <= np.size(fxs) <= 2:\n 335 |             raise ValueError(\n 336 |                 \"Each entry in features must be either an int, \"\n 337 |                 \"a string, or an iterable of size at most 2.\"\n 338 |             )\n 339 |         if kind != \"average\" and np.size(fxs) > 1:\n 340 |             raise ValueError(\n 341 |                 \"It is not possible to display individual effects for more \"\n 342 |                 f\"than one feature at a time. Got: features={features}.\"\n 343 |             )\n 344 |         tmp_features.append(fxs)\n 345 | \n 346 |     features = tmp_features\n 347 | \n 348 |     # Early exit if the axes does not have the correct number of axes\n 349 |     if ax is not None and not isinstance(ax, plt.Axes):\n 350 |         axes = np.asarray(ax, dtype=object)\n 351 |         if axes.size != len(features):\n 352 |             raise ValueError(\n 353 |                 \"Expected ax to have {} axes, got {}\".format(len(features), axes.size)\n 354 |             )\n 355 | \n 356 |     for i in chain.from_iterable(features):\n 357 |         if i >= len(feature_names):\n 358 |             raise ValueError(\n 359 |                 \"All entries of features must be less than \"\n 360 |                 \"len(feature_names) = {0}, got {1}.\".format(len(feature_names), i)\n 361 |             )\n 362 | \n 363 |     if isinstance(subsample, numbers.Integral):\n 364 |         if subsample <= 0:\n 365 |             raise ValueError(\n 366 |                 f\"When an integer, subsample={subsample} should be positive.\"\n 367 |             )\n 368 |     elif isinstance(subsample, numbers.Real):\n 369 |         if subsample <= 0 or subsample >= 1:\n 370 |             raise ValueError(\n 371 |                 f\"When a floating-point, subsample={subsample} should be in \"\n 372 |                 \"the (0, 1) range.\"\n 373 |             )\n 374 | \n 375 |     # compute predictions and/or averaged predictions\n 376 |     pd_results = Parallel(n_jobs=n_jobs, verbose=verbose)(\n 377 |         delayed(partial_dependence)(\n 378 |             estimator,\n 379 |             X,\n 380 |             fxs,\n 381 |             response_method=response_method,\n 382 |             method=method,\n 383 |             grid_resolution=grid_resolution,\n 384 |             percentiles=percentiles,\n 385 |             kind=kind,\n 386 |         )\n 387 |         for fxs in features\n 388 |     )\n 389 | \n 390 |     # For multioutput regression, we can only check the validity of target\n 391 |     # now that we have the predictions.\n 392 |     # Also note: as multiclass-multioutput classifiers are not supported,\n 393 |     # multiclass and multioutput scenario are mutually exclusive. So there is\n 394 |     # no risk of overwriting target_idx here.\n 395 |     pd_result = pd_results[0]  # checking the first result is enough\n 396 |     n_tasks = (\n 397 |         pd_result.average.shape[0]\n 398 |         if kind == \"average\"\n 399 |         else pd_result.individual.shape[0]\n 400 |     )\n 401 |     if is_regressor(estimator) and n_tasks > 1:\n 402 |         if target is None:\n 403 |             raise ValueError(\"target must be specified for multi-output regressors\")\n 404 |         if not 0 <= target <= n_tasks:\n 405 |             raise ValueError(\"target must be in [0, n_tasks], got {}.\".format(target))\n 406 |         target_idx = target\n 407 | \n 408 |     # get global min and max average predictions of PD grouped by plot type\n 409 |     pdp_lim = {}\n 410 |     for pdp in pd_results:\n 411 |         values = pdp[\"values\"]\n 412 |         preds = pdp.average if kind == \"average\" else pdp.individual\n 413 |         min_pd = preds[target_idx].min()\n 414 |         max_pd = preds[target_idx].max()\n 415 |         n_fx = len(values)\n 416 |         old_min_pd, old_max_pd = pdp_lim.get(n_fx, (min_pd, max_pd))\n 417 |         min_pd = min(min_pd, old_min_pd)\n 418 |         max_pd = max(max_pd, old_max_pd)\n 419 |         pdp_lim[n_fx] = (min_pd, max_pd)\n 420 | \n 421 |     deciles = {}\n 422 |     for fx in chain.from_iterable(features):\n 423 |         if fx not in deciles:\n 424 |             X_col = _safe_indexing(X, fx, axis=1)\n 425 |             deciles[fx] = mquantiles(X_col, prob=np.arange(0.1, 1.0, 0.1))\n 426 | \n 427 |     display = PartialDependenceDisplay(\n 428 |         pd_results=pd_results,\n 429 |         features=features,\n 430 |         feature_names=feature_names,\n 431 |         target_idx=target_idx,\n 432 |         pdp_lim=pdp_lim,\n 433 |         deciles=deciles,\n 434 |         kind=kind,\n 435 |         subsample=subsample,\n 436 |         random_state=random_state,\n 437 |     )\n 438 |     return display.plot(\n 439 |         ax=ax,\n 440 |         n_cols=n_cols,\n 441 |         line_kw=line_kw,\n 442 |         ice_lines_kw=ice_lines_kw,\n 443 |         pd_line_kw=pd_line_kw,\n 444 |         contour_kw=contour_kw,\n 445 |     )\n 446 | \n 447 | \n 448 | class PartialDependenceDisplay:\n 449 |     \"\"\"Partial Dependence Plot (PDP).\n 450 | \n 451 |     This can also display individual partial dependencies which are often\n 452 |     referred to as: Individual Condition Expectation (ICE).\n 453 | \n 454 |     It is recommended to use\n 455 |     :func:`~sklearn.inspection.plot_partial_dependence` to create a\n 456 |     :class:`~sklearn.inspection.PartialDependenceDisplay`. All parameters are\n 457 |     stored as attributes.\n 458 | \n 459 |     Read more in\n 460 |     :ref:`sphx_glr_auto_examples_miscellaneous_plot_partial_dependence_visualization_api.py`\n 461 |     and the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 462 | \n 463 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n 464 | \n 465 |     Parameters\n 466 |     ----------\n 467 |     pd_results : list of Bunch\n 468 |         Results of :func:`~sklearn.inspection.partial_dependence` for\n 469 |         ``features``.\n 470 | \n 471 |     features : list of (int,) or list of (int, int)\n 472 |         Indices of features for a given plot. A tuple of one integer will plot\n 473 |         a partial dependence curve of one feature. A tuple of two integers will\n 474 |         plot a two-way partial dependence curve as a contour plot.\n 475 | \n 476 |     feature_names : list of str\n 477 |         Feature names corresponding to the indices in ``features``.\n 478 | \n 479 |     target_idx : int\n 480 | \n 481 |         - In a multiclass setting, specifies the class for which the PDPs\n 482 |           should be computed. Note that for binary classification, the\n 483 |           positive class (index 1) is always used.\n 484 |         - In a multioutput setting, specifies the task for which the PDPs\n 485 |           should be computed.\n 486 | \n 487 |         Ignored in binary classification or classical regression settings.\n 488 | \n 489 |     pdp_lim : dict\n 490 |         Global min and max average predictions, such that all plots will have\n 491 |         the same scale and y limits. `pdp_lim[1]` is the global min and max for\n 492 |         single partial dependence curves. `pdp_lim[2]` is the global min and\n 493 |         max for two-way partial dependence curves.\n 494 | \n 495 |     deciles : dict\n 496 |         Deciles for feature indices in ``features``.\n 497 | \n 498 |     kind : {'average', 'individual', 'both'}, default='average'\n 499 |         Whether to plot the partial dependence averaged across all the samples\n 500 |         in the dataset or one line per sample or both.\n 501 | \n 502 |         - ``kind='average'`` results in the traditional PD plot;\n 503 |         - ``kind='individual'`` results in the ICE plot.\n 504 | \n 505 |        Note that the fast ``method='recursion'`` option is only available for\n 506 |        ``kind='average'``. Plotting individual dependencies requires using the\n 507 |        slower ``method='brute'`` option.\n 508 | \n 509 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 510 | \n 511 |     subsample : float, int or None, default=1000\n 512 |         Sampling for ICE curves when `kind` is 'individual' or 'both'.\n 513 |         If float, should be between 0.0 and 1.0 and represent the proportion\n 514 |         of the dataset to be used to plot ICE curves. If int, represents the\n 515 |         maximum absolute number of samples to use.\n 516 | \n 517 |         Note that the full dataset is still used to calculate partial\n 518 |         dependence when `kind='both'`.\n 519 | \n 520 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 521 | \n 522 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n 523 |         Controls the randomness of the selected samples when subsamples is not\n 524 |         `None`. See :term:`Glossary <random_state>` for details.\n 525 | \n 526 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 527 | \n 528 |     Attributes\n 529 |     ----------\n 530 |     bounding_ax_ : matplotlib Axes or None\n 531 |         If `ax` is an axes or None, the `bounding_ax_` is the axes where the\n 532 |         grid of partial dependence plots are drawn. If `ax` is a list of axes\n 533 |         or a numpy array of axes, `bounding_ax_` is None.\n 534 | \n 535 |     axes_ : ndarray of matplotlib Axes\n 536 |         If `ax` is an axes or None, `axes_[i, j]` is the axes on the i-th row\n 537 |         and j-th column. If `ax` is a list of axes, `axes_[i]` is the i-th item\n 538 |         in `ax`. Elements that are None correspond to a nonexisting axes in\n 539 |         that position.\n 540 | \n 541 |     lines_ : ndarray of matplotlib Artists\n 542 |         If `ax` is an axes or None, `lines_[i, j]` is the partial dependence\n 543 |         curve on the i-th row and j-th column. If `ax` is a list of axes,\n 544 |         `lines_[i]` is the partial dependence curve corresponding to the i-th\n 545 |         item in `ax`. Elements that are None correspond to a nonexisting axes\n 546 |         or an axes that does not include a line plot.\n 547 | \n 548 |     deciles_vlines_ : ndarray of matplotlib LineCollection\n 549 |         If `ax` is an axes or None, `vlines_[i, j]` is the line collection\n 550 |         representing the x axis deciles of the i-th row and j-th column. If\n 551 |         `ax` is a list of axes, `vlines_[i]` corresponds to the i-th item in\n 552 |         `ax`. Elements that are None correspond to a nonexisting axes or an\n 553 |         axes that does not include a PDP plot.\n 554 | \n 555 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 556 | \n 557 |     deciles_hlines_ : ndarray of matplotlib LineCollection\n 558 |         If `ax` is an axes or None, `vlines_[i, j]` is the line collection\n 559 |         representing the y axis deciles of the i-th row and j-th column. If\n 560 |         `ax` is a list of axes, `vlines_[i]` corresponds to the i-th item in\n 561 |         `ax`. Elements that are None correspond to a nonexisting axes or an\n 562 |         axes that does not include a 2-way plot.\n 563 | \n 564 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n 565 | \n 566 |     contours_ : ndarray of matplotlib Artists\n 567 |         If `ax` is an axes or None, `contours_[i, j]` is the partial dependence\n 568 |         plot on the i-th row and j-th column. If `ax` is a list of axes,\n 569 |         `contours_[i]` is the partial dependence plot corresponding to the i-th\n 570 |         item in `ax`. Elements that are None correspond to a nonexisting axes\n 571 |         or an axes that does not include a contour plot.\n 572 | \n 573 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 574 |         Figure containing partial dependence plots.\n 575 | \n 576 |     See Also\n 577 |     --------\n 578 |     partial_dependence : Compute Partial Dependence values.\n 579 |     plot_partial_dependence : Plot Partial Dependence.\n 580 |     \"\"\"\n 581 | \n 582 |     def __init__(\n 583 |         self,\n 584 |         pd_results,\n 585 |         *,\n 586 |         features,\n 587 |         feature_names,\n 588 |         target_idx,\n 589 |         pdp_lim,\n 590 |         deciles,\n 591 |         kind=\"average\",\n 592 |         subsample=1000,\n 593 |         random_state=None,\n 594 |     ):\n 595 |         self.pd_results = pd_results\n 596 |         self.features = features\n 597 |         self.feature_names = feature_names\n 598 |         self.target_idx = target_idx\n 599 |         self.pdp_lim = pdp_lim\n 600 |         self.deciles = deciles\n 601 |         self.kind = kind\n 602 |         self.subsample = subsample\n 603 |         self.random_state = random_state\n 604 | \n 605 |     def _get_sample_count(self, n_samples):\n 606 |         \"\"\"Compute the number of samples as an integer.\"\"\"\n 607 |         if isinstance(self.subsample, numbers.Integral):\n 608 |             if self.subsample < n_samples:\n 609 |                 return self.subsample\n 610 |             return n_samples\n 611 |         elif isinstance(self.subsample, numbers.Real):\n 612 |             return ceil(n_samples * self.subsample)\n 613 |         return n_samples\n 614 | \n 615 |     def _plot_ice_lines(\n 616 |         self,\n 617 |         preds,\n 618 |         feature_values,\n 619 |         n_ice_to_plot,\n 620 |         ax,\n 621 |         pd_plot_idx,\n 622 |         n_total_lines_by_plot,\n 623 |         individual_line_kw,\n 624 |     ):\n 625 |         \"\"\"Plot the ICE lines.\n 626 | \n 627 |         Parameters\n 628 |         ----------\n 629 |         preds : ndarray of shape \\\n 630 |                 (n_instances, n_grid_points)\n 631 |             The predictions computed for all points of `feature_values` for a\n 632 |             given feature for all samples in `X`.\n 633 |         feature_values : ndarray of shape (n_grid_points,)\n 634 |             The feature values for which the predictions have been computed.\n 635 |         n_ice_to_plot : int\n 636 |             The number of ICE lines to plot.\n 637 |         ax : Matplotlib axes\n 638 |             The axis on which to plot the ICE lines.\n 639 |         pd_plot_idx : int\n 640 |             The sequential index of the plot. It will be unraveled to find the\n 641 |             matching 2D position in the grid layout.\n 642 |         n_total_lines_by_plot : int\n 643 |             The total number of lines expected to be plot on the axis.\n 644 |         individual_line_kw : dict\n 645 |             Dict with keywords passed when plotting the ICE lines.\n 646 |         \"\"\"\n 647 |         rng = check_random_state(self.random_state)\n 648 |         # subsample ice\n 649 |         ice_lines_idx = rng.choice(\n 650 |             preds.shape[0],\n 651 |             n_ice_to_plot,\n 652 |             replace=False,\n 653 |         )\n 654 |         ice_lines_subsampled = preds[ice_lines_idx, :]\n 655 |         # plot the subsampled ice\n 656 |         for ice_idx, ice in enumerate(ice_lines_subsampled):\n 657 |             line_idx = np.unravel_index(\n 658 |                 pd_plot_idx * n_total_lines_by_plot + ice_idx, self.lines_.shape\n 659 |             )\n 660 |             self.lines_[line_idx] = ax.plot(\n 661 |                 feature_values, ice.ravel(), **individual_line_kw\n 662 |             )[0]\n 663 | \n 664 |     def _plot_average_dependence(\n 665 |         self,\n 666 |         avg_preds,\n 667 |         feature_values,\n 668 |         ax,\n 669 |         pd_line_idx,\n 670 |         line_kw,\n 671 |     ):\n 672 |         \"\"\"Plot the average partial dependence.\n 673 | \n 674 |         Parameters\n 675 |         ----------\n 676 |         avg_preds : ndarray of shape (n_grid_points,)\n 677 |             The average predictions for all points of `feature_values` for a\n 678 |             given feature for all samples in `X`.\n 679 |         feature_values : ndarray of shape (n_grid_points,)\n 680 |             The feature values for which the predictions have been computed.\n 681 |         ax : Matplotlib axes\n 682 |             The axis on which to plot the ICE lines.\n 683 |         pd_line_idx : int\n 684 |             The sequential index of the plot. It will be unraveled to find the\n 685 |             matching 2D position in the grid layout.\n 686 |         line_kw : dict\n 687 |             Dict with keywords passed when plotting the PD plot.\n 688 |         \"\"\"\n 689 |         line_idx = np.unravel_index(pd_line_idx, self.lines_.shape)\n 690 |         self.lines_[line_idx] = ax.plot(\n 691 |             feature_values,\n 692 |             avg_preds,\n 693 |             **line_kw,\n 694 |         )[0]\n 695 | \n 696 |     def _plot_one_way_partial_dependence(\n 697 |         self,\n 698 |         preds,\n 699 |         avg_preds,\n 700 |         feature_values,\n 701 |         feature_idx,\n 702 |         n_ice_lines,\n 703 |         ax,\n 704 |         n_cols,\n 705 |         pd_plot_idx,\n 706 |         n_lines,\n 707 |         ice_lines_kw,\n 708 |         pd_line_kw,\n 709 |     ):\n 710 |         \"\"\"Plot 1-way partial dependence: ICE and PDP.\n 711 | \n 712 |         Parameters\n 713 |         ----------\n 714 |         preds : ndarray of shape \\\n 715 |                 (n_instances, n_grid_points) or None\n 716 |             The predictions computed for all points of `feature_values` for a\n 717 |             given feature for all samples in `X`.\n 718 |         avg_preds : ndarray of shape (n_grid_points,)\n 719 |             The average predictions for all points of `feature_values` for a\n 720 |             given feature for all samples in `X`.\n 721 |         feature_values : ndarray of shape (n_grid_points,)\n 722 |             The feature values for which the predictions have been computed.\n 723 |         feature_idx : int\n 724 |             The index corresponding to the target feature.\n 725 |         n_ice_lines : int\n 726 |             The number of ICE lines to plot.\n 727 |         ax : Matplotlib axes\n 728 |             The axis on which to plot the ICE and PDP lines.\n 729 |         n_cols : int or None\n 730 |             The number of column in the axis.\n 731 |         pd_plot_idx : int\n 732 |             The sequential index of the plot. It will be unraveled to find the\n 733 |             matching 2D position in the grid layout.\n 734 |         n_lines : int\n 735 |             The total number of lines expected to be plot on the axis.\n 736 |         ice_lines_kw : dict\n 737 |             Dict with keywords passed when plotting the ICE lines.\n 738 |         pd_line_kw : dict\n 739 |             Dict with keywords passed when plotting the PD plot.\n 740 |         \"\"\"\n 741 |         from matplotlib import transforms  # noqa\n 742 | \n 743 |         if self.kind in (\"individual\", \"both\"):\n 744 |             self._plot_ice_lines(\n 745 |                 preds[self.target_idx],\n 746 |                 feature_values,\n 747 |                 n_ice_lines,\n 748 |                 ax,\n 749 |                 pd_plot_idx,\n 750 |                 n_lines,\n 751 |                 ice_lines_kw,\n 752 |             )\n 753 | \n 754 |         if self.kind in (\"average\", \"both\"):\n 755 |             # the average is stored as the last line\n 756 |             if self.kind == \"average\":\n 757 |                 pd_line_idx = pd_plot_idx\n 758 |             else:\n 759 |                 pd_line_idx = pd_plot_idx * n_lines + n_ice_lines\n 760 |             self._plot_average_dependence(\n 761 |                 avg_preds[self.target_idx].ravel(),\n 762 |                 feature_values,\n 763 |                 ax,\n 764 |                 pd_line_idx,\n 765 |                 pd_line_kw,\n 766 |             )\n 767 | \n 768 |         trans = transforms.blended_transform_factory(ax.transData, ax.transAxes)\n 769 |         # create the decile line for the vertical axis\n 770 |         vlines_idx = np.unravel_index(pd_plot_idx, self.deciles_vlines_.shape)\n 771 |         self.deciles_vlines_[vlines_idx] = ax.vlines(\n 772 |             self.deciles[feature_idx[0]],\n 773 |             0,\n 774 |             0.05,\n 775 |             transform=trans,\n 776 |             color=\"k\",\n 777 |         )\n 778 |         # reset ylim which was overwritten by vlines\n 779 |         ax.set_ylim(self.pdp_lim[1])\n 780 | \n 781 |         # Set xlabel if it is not already set\n 782 |         if not ax.get_xlabel():\n 783 |             ax.set_xlabel(self.feature_names[feature_idx[0]])\n 784 | \n 785 |         if n_cols is None or pd_plot_idx % n_cols == 0:\n 786 |             if not ax.get_ylabel():\n 787 |                 ax.set_ylabel(\"Partial dependence\")\n 788 |         else:\n 789 |             ax.set_yticklabels([])\n 790 | \n 791 |         if pd_line_kw.get(\"label\", None) and self.kind != \"individual\":\n 792 |             ax.legend()\n 793 | \n 794 |     def _plot_two_way_partial_dependence(\n 795 |         self,\n 796 |         avg_preds,\n 797 |         feature_values,\n 798 |         feature_idx,\n 799 |         ax,\n 800 |         pd_plot_idx,\n 801 |         Z_level,\n 802 |         contour_kw,\n 803 |     ):\n 804 |         \"\"\"Plot 2-way partial dependence.\n 805 | \n 806 |         Parameters\n 807 |         ----------\n 808 |         avg_preds : ndarray of shape \\\n 809 |                 (n_instances, n_grid_points, n_grid_points)\n 810 |             The average predictions for all points of `feature_values[0]` and\n 811 |             `feature_values[1]` for some given features for all samples in `X`.\n 812 |         feature_values : seq of 1d array\n 813 |             A sequence of array of the feature values for which the predictions\n 814 |             have been computed.\n 815 |         feature_idx : tuple of int\n 816 |             The indices of the target features\n 817 |         ax : Matplotlib axes\n 818 |             The axis on which to plot the ICE and PDP lines.\n 819 |         pd_plot_idx : int\n 820 |             The sequential index of the plot. It will be unraveled to find the\n 821 |             matching 2D position in the grid layout.\n 822 |         Z_level : ndarray of shape (8, 8)\n 823 |             The Z-level used to encode the average predictions.\n 824 |         contour_kw : dict\n 825 |             Dict with keywords passed when plotting the contours.\n 826 |         \"\"\"\n 827 |         from matplotlib import transforms  # noqa\n 828 | \n 829 |         XX, YY = np.meshgrid(feature_values[0], feature_values[1])\n 830 |         Z = avg_preds[self.target_idx].T\n 831 |         CS = ax.contour(XX, YY, Z, levels=Z_level, linewidths=0.5, colors=\"k\")\n 832 |         contour_idx = np.unravel_index(pd_plot_idx, self.contours_.shape)\n 833 |         self.contours_[contour_idx] = ax.contourf(\n 834 |             XX,\n 835 |             YY,\n 836 |             Z,\n 837 |             levels=Z_level,\n 838 |             vmax=Z_level[-1],\n 839 |             vmin=Z_level[0],\n 840 |             **contour_kw,\n 841 |         )\n 842 |         ax.clabel(CS, fmt=\"%2.2f\", colors=\"k\", fontsize=10, inline=True)\n 843 | \n 844 |         trans = transforms.blended_transform_factory(ax.transData, ax.transAxes)\n 845 |         # create the decile line for the vertical axis\n 846 |         xlim, ylim = ax.get_xlim(), ax.get_ylim()\n 847 |         vlines_idx = np.unravel_index(pd_plot_idx, self.deciles_vlines_.shape)\n 848 |         self.deciles_vlines_[vlines_idx] = ax.vlines(\n 849 |             self.deciles[feature_idx[0]],\n 850 |             0,\n 851 |             0.05,\n 852 |             transform=trans,\n 853 |             color=\"k\",\n 854 |         )\n 855 |         # create the decile line for the horizontal axis\n 856 |         hlines_idx = np.unravel_index(pd_plot_idx, self.deciles_hlines_.shape)\n 857 |         self.deciles_hlines_[hlines_idx] = ax.hlines(\n 858 |             self.deciles[feature_idx[1]],\n 859 |             0,\n 860 |             0.05,\n 861 |             transform=trans,\n 862 |             color=\"k\",\n 863 |         )\n 864 |         # reset xlim and ylim since they are overwritten by hlines and vlines\n 865 |         ax.set_xlim(xlim)\n 866 |         ax.set_ylim(ylim)\n 867 | \n 868 |         # set xlabel if it is not already set\n 869 |         if not ax.get_xlabel():\n 870 |             ax.set_xlabel(self.feature_names[feature_idx[0]])\n 871 |         ax.set_ylabel(self.feature_names[feature_idx[1]])\n 872 | \n 873 |     @_deprecate_positional_args(version=\"1.1\")\n 874 |     def plot(\n 875 |         self,\n 876 |         *,\n 877 |         ax=None,\n 878 |         n_cols=3,\n 879 |         line_kw=None,\n 880 |         ice_lines_kw=None,\n 881 |         pd_line_kw=None,\n 882 |         contour_kw=None,\n 883 |     ):\n 884 |         \"\"\"Plot partial dependence plots.\n 885 | \n 886 |         Parameters\n 887 |         ----------\n 888 |         ax : Matplotlib axes or array-like of Matplotlib axes, default=None\n 889 |             - If a single axis is passed in, it is treated as a bounding axes\n 890 |                 and a grid of partial dependence plots will be drawn within\n 891 |                 these bounds. The `n_cols` parameter controls the number of\n 892 |                 columns in the grid.\n 893 |             - If an array-like of axes are passed in, the partial dependence\n 894 |                 plots will be drawn directly into these axes.\n 895 |             - If `None`, a figure and a bounding axes is created and treated\n 896 |                 as the single axes case.\n 897 | \n 898 |         n_cols : int, default=3\n 899 |             The maximum number of columns in the grid plot. Only active when\n 900 |             `ax` is a single axes or `None`.\n 901 | \n 902 |         line_kw : dict, default=None\n 903 |             Dict with keywords passed to the `matplotlib.pyplot.plot` call.\n 904 |             For one-way partial dependence plots.\n 905 | \n 906 |         ice_lines_kw : dict, default=None\n 907 |             Dictionary with keywords passed to the `matplotlib.pyplot.plot` call.\n 908 |             For ICE lines in the one-way partial dependence plots.\n 909 |             The key value pairs defined in `ice_lines_kw` takes priority over\n 910 |             `line_kw`.\n 911 | \n 912 |             .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 913 | \n 914 |         pd_line_kw : dict, default=None\n 915 |             Dictionary with keywords passed to the `matplotlib.pyplot.plot` call.\n 916 |             For partial dependence in one-way partial dependence plots.\n 917 |             The key value pairs defined in `pd_line_kw` takes priority over\n 918 |             `line_kw`.\n 919 | \n 920 |             .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 921 | \n 922 |         contour_kw : dict, default=None\n 923 |             Dict with keywords passed to the `matplotlib.pyplot.contourf`\n 924 |             call for two-way partial dependence plots.\n 925 | \n 926 |         Returns\n 927 |         -------\n 928 |         display : :class:`~sklearn.inspection.PartialDependenceDisplay`\n 929 |         \"\"\"\n 930 | \n 931 |         check_matplotlib_support(\"plot_partial_dependence\")\n 932 |         import matplotlib.pyplot as plt  # noqa\n 933 |         from matplotlib.gridspec import GridSpecFromSubplotSpec  # noqa\n 934 | \n 935 |         if line_kw is None:\n 936 |             line_kw = {}\n 937 |         if ice_lines_kw is None:\n 938 |             ice_lines_kw = {}\n 939 |         if pd_line_kw is None:\n 940 |             pd_line_kw = {}\n 941 |         if contour_kw is None:\n 942 |             contour_kw = {}\n 943 | \n 944 |         if ax is None:\n 945 |             _, ax = plt.subplots()\n 946 | \n 947 |         default_contour_kws = {\"alpha\": 0.75}\n 948 |         contour_kw = {**default_contour_kws, **contour_kw}\n 949 | \n 950 |         default_line_kws = {\n 951 |             \"color\": \"C0\",\n 952 |             \"label\": \"average\" if self.kind == \"both\" else None,\n 953 |         }\n 954 |         if self.kind in (\"individual\", \"both\"):\n 955 |             default_ice_lines_kws = {\"alpha\": 0.3, \"linewidth\": 0.5}\n 956 |         else:\n 957 |             default_ice_lines_kws = {}\n 958 | \n 959 |         ice_lines_kw = {\n 960 |             **default_line_kws,\n 961 |             **line_kw,\n 962 |             **default_ice_lines_kws,\n 963 |             **ice_lines_kw,\n 964 |         }\n 965 |         del ice_lines_kw[\"label\"]\n 966 | \n 967 |         pd_line_kw = {**default_line_kws, **line_kw, **pd_line_kw}\n 968 | \n 969 |         n_features = len(self.features)\n 970 |         if self.kind in (\"individual\", \"both\"):\n 971 |             n_ice_lines = self._get_sample_count(len(self.pd_results[0].individual[0]))\n 972 |             if self.kind == \"individual\":\n 973 |                 n_lines = n_ice_lines\n 974 |             else:\n 975 |                 n_lines = n_ice_lines + 1\n 976 |         else:\n 977 |             n_ice_lines = 0\n 978 |             n_lines = 1\n 979 | \n 980 |         if isinstance(ax, plt.Axes):\n 981 |             # If ax was set off, it has most likely been set to off\n 982 |             # by a previous call to plot.\n 983 |             if not ax.axison:\n 984 |                 raise ValueError(\n 985 |                     \"The ax was already used in another plot \"\n 986 |                     \"function, please set ax=display.axes_ \"\n 987 |                     \"instead\"\n 988 |                 )\n 989 | \n 990 |             ax.set_axis_off()\n 991 |             self.bounding_ax_ = ax\n 992 |             self.figure_ = ax.figure\n 993 | \n 994 |             n_cols = min(n_cols, n_features)\n 995 |             n_rows = int(np.ceil(n_features / float(n_cols)))\n 996 | \n 997 |             self.axes_ = np.empty((n_rows, n_cols), dtype=object)\n 998 |             if self.kind == \"average\":\n 999 |                 self.lines_ = np.empty((n_rows, n_cols), dtype=object)\n1000 |             else:\n1001 |                 self.lines_ = np.empty((n_rows, n_cols, n_lines), dtype=object)\n1002 |             self.contours_ = np.empty((n_rows, n_cols), dtype=object)\n1003 | \n1004 |             axes_ravel = self.axes_.ravel()\n1005 | \n1006 |             gs = GridSpecFromSubplotSpec(\n1007 |                 n_rows, n_cols, subplot_spec=ax.get_subplotspec()\n1008 |             )\n1009 |             for i, spec in zip(range(n_features), gs):\n1010 |                 axes_ravel[i] = self.figure_.add_subplot(spec)\n1011 | \n1012 |         else:  # array-like\n1013 |             ax = np.asarray(ax, dtype=object)\n1014 |             if ax.size != n_features:\n1015 |                 raise ValueError(\n1016 |                     \"Expected ax to have {} axes, got {}\".format(n_features, ax.size)\n1017 |                 )\n1018 | \n1019 |             if ax.ndim == 2:\n1020 |                 n_cols = ax.shape[1]\n1021 |             else:\n1022 |                 n_cols = None\n1023 | \n1024 |             self.bounding_ax_ = None\n1025 |             self.figure_ = ax.ravel()[0].figure\n1026 |             self.axes_ = ax\n1027 |             if self.kind == \"average\":\n1028 |                 self.lines_ = np.empty_like(ax, dtype=object)\n1029 |             else:\n1030 |                 self.lines_ = np.empty(ax.shape + (n_lines,), dtype=object)\n1031 |             self.contours_ = np.empty_like(ax, dtype=object)\n1032 | \n1033 |         # create contour levels for two-way plots\n1034 |         if 2 in self.pdp_lim:\n1035 |             Z_level = np.linspace(*self.pdp_lim[2], num=8)\n1036 | \n1037 |         self.deciles_vlines_ = np.empty_like(self.axes_, dtype=object)\n1038 |         self.deciles_hlines_ = np.empty_like(self.axes_, dtype=object)\n1039 | \n1040 |         for pd_plot_idx, (axi, feature_idx, pd_result) in enumerate(\n1041 |             zip(self.axes_.ravel(), self.features, self.pd_results)\n1042 |         ):\n1043 |             avg_preds = None\n1044 |             preds = None\n1045 |             feature_values = pd_result[\"values\"]\n1046 |             if self.kind == \"individual\":\n1047 |                 preds = pd_result.individual\n1048 |             elif self.kind == \"average\":\n1049 |                 avg_preds = pd_result.average\n1050 |             else:  # kind='both'\n1051 |                 avg_preds = pd_result.average\n1052 |                 preds = pd_result.individual\n1053 | \n1054 |             if len(feature_values) == 1:\n1055 |                 self._plot_one_way_partial_dependence(\n1056 |                     preds,\n1057 |                     avg_preds,\n1058 |                     feature_values[0],\n1059 |                     feature_idx,\n1060 |                     n_ice_lines,\n1061 |                     axi,\n1062 |                     n_cols,\n1063 |                     pd_plot_idx,\n1064 |                     n_lines,\n1065 |                     ice_lines_kw,\n1066 |                     pd_line_kw,\n1067 |                 )\n1068 |             else:\n1069 |                 self._plot_two_way_partial_dependence(\n1070 |                     avg_preds,\n1071 |                     feature_values,\n1072 |                     feature_idx,\n1073 |                     axi,\n1074 |                     pd_plot_idx,\n1075 |                     Z_level,\n1076 |                     contour_kw,\n1077 |                 )\n1078 | \n1079 |         return self\n1080 | \n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\n```\n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the search results, the `calibration_curve` function is already implemented in `sklearn/calibration.py`. To add the `plot_calibration_curve` function, we should integrate it into the existing plotting infrastructure. Given that `sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py` contains several plotting functions and a class `PartialDependenceDisplay`, it makes sense to add the `plot_calibration_curve` function to a similar plotting module or create a new one if it's deemed appropriate.\n\nFor simplicity and consistency, let's add the `plot_calibration_curve` function to `sklearn/inspection/_plot`. We'll place it alongside other plotting functions and create a new class if necessary.\n\nHere are the potential locations for the new feature:\n\n```\nsklearn/inspection/_plot/__init__.py\nline: 1\n\nsklearn/inspection/_plot/base.py\nline: 1\n\nsklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py\nline: 1\n\nsklearn/inspection/_plot/calibration_curve.py\nline: 1\n\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 840\nfunction: calibration_curve\n```\n\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 144894, "completion_tokens": 611}, "time": 138.0084490776062}]}}
