13131 |             Include only `float`, `int` or `boolean` data.\n13132 | \n13133 |             .. versionchanged:: 2.0.0\n13134 |                 The default value of ``numeric_only`` is now ``False``.\n13135 | \n13136 |         interpolation : {'linear', 'lower', 'higher', 'midpoint', 'nearest'}\n13137 |             This optional parameter specifies the interpolation method to use,\n13138 |             when the desired quantile lies between two data points `i` and `j`:\n13139 | \n13140 |             * linear: `i + (j - i) * fraction`, where `fraction` is the\n13141 |               fractional part of the index surrounded by `i` and `j`.\n13142 |             * lower: `i`.\n13143 |             * higher: `j`.\n13144 |             * nearest: `i` or `j` whichever is nearest.\n13145 |             * midpoint: (`i` + `j`) / 2.\n13146 |         method : {'single', 'table'}, default 'single'\n13147 |             Whether to compute quantiles per-column ('single') or over all columns\n13148 |             ('table'). When 'table', the only allowed interpolation methods are\n13149 |             'nearest', 'lower', and 'higher'.\n13150 | \n13151 |         Returns\n13152 |         -------\n13153 |         Series or DataFrame\n13154 | \n13155 |             If ``q`` is an array, a DataFrame will be returned where the\n13156 |               index is ``q``, the columns are the columns of self, and the\n13157 |               values are the quantiles.\n13158 |             If ``q`` is a float, a Series will be returned where the\n13159 |               index is the columns of self and the values are the quantiles.\n13160 | \n13161 |         See Also\n13162 |         --------\n13163 |         core.window.rolling.Rolling.quantile: Rolling quantile.\n13164 |         numpy.percentile: Numpy function to compute the percentile.\n13165 | \n13166 |         Examples\n13167 |         --------\n13168 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n13169 |         ...     np.array([[1, 1], [2, 10], [3, 100], [4, 100]]), columns=[\"a\", \"b\"]\n13170 |         ... )\n13171 |         >>> df.quantile(0.1)\n13172 |         a    1.3\n13173 |         b    3.7\n13174 |         Name: 0.1, dtype: float64\n13175 |         >>> df.quantile([0.1, 0.5])\n13176 |                a     b\n13177 |         0.1  1.3   3.7\n13178 |         0.5  2.5  55.0\n13179 | \n13180 |         Specifying `method='table'` will compute the quantile over all columns.\n13181 | \n13182 |         >>> df.quantile(0.1, method=\"table\", interpolation=\"nearest\")\n13183 |         a    1\n13184 |         b    1\n13185 |         Name: 0.1, dtype: int64\n13186 |         >>> df.quantile([0.1, 0.5], method=\"table\", interpolation=\"nearest\")\n13187 |              a    b\n13188 |         0.1  1    1\n13189 |         0.5  3  100\n13190 | \n13191 |         Specifying `numeric_only=False` will also compute the quantile of\n13192 |         datetime and timedelta data.\n13193 | \n13194 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n13195 |         ...     {\n13196 |         ...         \"A\": [1, 2],\n13197 |         ...         \"B\": [pd.Timestamp(\"2010\"), pd.Timestamp(\"2011\")],\n13198 |         ...         \"C\": [pd.Timedelta(\"1 days\"), pd.Timedelta(\"2 days\")],\n13199 |         ...     }\n13200 |         ... )\n13201 |         >>> df.quantile(0.5, numeric_only=False)\n13202 |         A                    1.5\n13203 |         B    2010-07-02 12:00:00\n13204 |         C        1 days 12:00:00\n13205 |         Name: 0.5, dtype: object\n13206 |         \"\"\"\n13207 |         validate_percentile(q)\n13208 |         axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n13209 | \n13210 |         if not is_list_like(q):\n13211 |             # BlockManager.quantile expects listlike, so we wrap and unwrap here\n13212 |             # error: List item 0 has incompatible type \"float | ExtensionArray |\n13213 |             # ndarray[Any, Any] | Index | Series | Sequence[float]\"; expected \"float\"\n13214 |             res_df = self.quantile(\n13215 |   