: Return the counts of values in a Series.\n13018 | \n13019 |         Examples\n13020 |         --------\n13021 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n13022 |         ...     [\n13023 |         ...         (\"bird\", 2, 2),\n13024 |         ...         (\"mammal\", 4, np.nan),\n13025 |         ...         (\"arthropod\", 8, 0),\n13026 |         ...         (\"bird\", 2, np.nan),\n13027 |         ...     ],\n13028 |         ...     index=(\"falcon\", \"horse\", \"spider\", \"ostrich\"),\n13029 |         ...     columns=(\"species\", \"legs\", \"wings\"),\n13030 |         ... )\n13031 |         >>> df\n13032 |                    species  legs  wings\n13033 |         falcon        bird     2    2.0\n13034 |         horse       mammal     4    NaN\n13035 |         spider   arthropod     8    0.0\n13036 |         ostrich       bird     2    NaN\n13037 | \n13038 |         By default, missing values are not considered, and the mode of wings\n13039 |         are both 0 and 2. Because the resulting DataFrame has two rows,\n13040 |         the second row of ``species`` and ``legs`` contains ``NaN``.\n13041 | \n13042 |         >>> df.mode()\n13043 |           species  legs  wings\n13044 |         0    bird   2.0    0.0\n13045 |         1     NaN   NaN    2.0\n13046 | \n13047 |         Setting ``dropna=False`` ``NaN`` values are considered and they can be\n13048 |         the mode (like for wings).\n13049 | \n13050 |         >>> df.mode(dropna=False)\n13051 |           species  legs  wings\n13052 |         0    bird     2    NaN\n13053 | \n13054 |         Setting ``numeric_only=True``, only the mode of numeric columns is\n13055 |         computed, and columns of other types are ignored.\n13056 | \n13057 |         >>> df.mode(numeric_only=True)\n13058 |            legs  wings\n13059 |         0   2.0    0.0\n13060 |         1   NaN    2.0\n13061 | \n13062 |         To compute the mode over columns and not rows, use the axis parameter:\n13063 | \n13064 |         >>> df.mode(axis=\"col